WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.230 --> 00:00:02.230
Tim Ferriss: Hello, my friends and frenemies.

00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:03.230
This is Tim Ferriss and welcome to another
episode of The Tim Ferriss Show, where it

00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:04.230
is my job to deconstruct world-class performers,
to tease out the habits, routines, tools,

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:05.230
favorite books, etc. that you can use and
test and apply to your own life.

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:06.230
The topics we explore the experts we interview
range from people in the military to entertainment,

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:07.230
sports, and everything in between.

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:08.230
This is an everything-in-between episode.

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:09.230
My guest is Jason Nemer, co-founder of AcroYoga.

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:10.230
You can find him on Instagram @jasonnemer,
N-E-M-E-R.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:11.230
My apologies for the sucking in the background.

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:12.230
That’s my dog obliterating a Kong.

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:13.230
This is audio vérité, for you folks.

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:14.230
Jason is an incredible character.

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:15.230
He’s a vagabond.

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:16.230
He travels the world with next to nothing
and has introduced me to my obsession for

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:17.230
the last 6 to 12 months.

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:18.230
Now, along with gymnastic strength training,
I’ve been doing AcroYoga.

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:19.230
I think about doing it all the time.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:20.230
It is not what you think in your mind of as
yoga.

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:21.230
It is totally different because I, of course,
have found yoga, historically for me, very

00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:22.230
boring and I always strain a hamstring.

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:23.230
It’s not fun.

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:24.230
This is the opposite of all of those things.

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:25.230
It is closer to partner acrobatics, a Cirque
du Soleil routine meets sensual, but not sexual,

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:26.230
contact.

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:27.230
If that makes any sense.

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:28.230
If it doesn’t, it will in this episode.

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:29.230
There are lots of takeaways, lots of recommendations.

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:30.230
It was also videotaped.

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:31.230
We do a couple of demos.

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:32.230
You do not need the video, because we walk
through it in audio.

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:33.230
So you can definitely just listen to this
podcast.

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:34.230
But if you want to see some of the demos visually,
you can go to Youtube.com/timferriss to see

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:35.230
it.

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:36.230
It was filmed at CreativeLive, my favorite
place to learn online.

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:37.230
Creativelive.com.

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:38.230
Go to creativelive.com/success – I asked
them of all of their courses, they have hundreds,

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:39.230
I’ve done a lot with them – what would
they recommend that I share with you guys

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:40.230
and they brought up Six Months to Success,
which is actually Six Months to Six Figures

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:41.230
by Peter Voogd.

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:42.230
So Six Months to Six Figures by Peter Voogd,
V-O-O-G-D.

00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:43.230
It has incredible reviews.

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:44.230
Just check out creativelive.com/success.

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:45.230
But I want to keep this as short as possible.

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:46.230
It’s already long because I had a lot of
caffeine and I’ve done a lot of thinking

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:47.230
on AcroYoga today.

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:48.230
When I’m not doing it, I want to be doing
it.

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:49.230
By the end of this episode, you will know
why and hopefully you will get a chance to

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:50.230
try it yourself.

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:51.230
So please enjoy.

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:52.230
Jason, welcome to the show.

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:53.230
Jason Nemer: Thank you.

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:54.230
Tim Ferriss: What are we drinking, here?

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:55.230
Jason Nemer: What a great way to start.

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:56.230
Tim Ferriss: I enjoyed the label so much I
have to share it with the world.

00:00:56.230 --> 00:00:57.230
Jason Nemer: It’s Duck Shit Oolong Tea.

00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:58.230
Tim Ferriss: Duck Shit Oolong Tea.

00:00:58.230 --> 00:00:59.230
Jason Nemer: It’s true.

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:00.230
Tim Ferriss: What is the story behind the
Duck Shit portion of that?

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:01.230
Jason Nemer: Back in the day, I hear, in a
region in China there was this amazing tea

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:02.230
and it was so good that they had to play it
down so they called it Duck Shit tea, and

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:03.230
it was played down for however many centuries
until it’s been discovered as an excellent

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:04.230
tea.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:05.230
Tim Ferriss: It’s not bad.

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:06.230
I’ve got to say I’m enjoying the Duck
Shit fragrance oolong tea.

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:07.230
It is almost as odd sounding as our first
encounter.

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:08.230
I remember the dinner at which we met, and
this was in LA, probably Venice, maybe Santa

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:09.230
Monica.

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:10.230
Jason Nemer: Santa Monica.

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:11.230
Tim Ferriss: It was at whose home?

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:12.230
Was it Andrew’s?

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:13.230
Jason Nemer: Dean’s home.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:14.230
Tim Ferriss: Dean.

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:15.230
I was with my buddy Travis Brewer.

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:16.230
Jason Nemer: The ninja.

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:17.230
Tim Ferriss: The ninja.

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:18.230
Who is very much worth checking out for those
people who have not seen him in action; American

00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:19.230
Ninja Warrior and so on.

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:20.230
We were sitting somewhere close to each other,
maybe next to each other.

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:21.230
Jason Nemer: We’d captured a bunch of photographs
of the day, just running around doing craziness.

00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:22.230
Tim Ferriss: That’s right.

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:25.390
At the Santa Monica Green and elsewhere.

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:30.450
Of course, you are the co-founder of AcroYoga,
but I didn’t have much in terms of exposure

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:31.450
to AcroYoga.

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:32.450
So I said, “What is AcroYoga?”

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:34.310
You said, “I’ll show you.”

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:40.229
That led to an Instagram photo that I posted,
which was something along the lines of “Just

00:01:40.229 --> 00:01:43.689
another Saturday night, not sure how I ended
up here.”

00:01:43.689 --> 00:01:45.759
And I was in reverse bat position.

00:01:45.759 --> 00:01:46.869
Jason Nemer: Well done.

00:01:46.869 --> 00:01:47.869
You know exactly the pose.

00:01:47.869 --> 00:01:50.110
Tim Ferriss: In what I used to call butter
kinase.

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:51.259
Jason Nemer: Baddha konasana.

00:01:51.259 --> 00:01:52.420
Tim Ferriss: Baddha konasana.

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:59.590
I’m not good with the such-and-such onasana
– memorization of terms.

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:00.700
What is that?

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:02.510
How would you describe what that looks like?

00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:04.960
Maybe we’ll do a quick demo on what that
looks like.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:08.950
Jason Nemer: Basically if you were a human
bat and you were hanging upside down and you

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:11.410
were holding your ankles, that’s pretty
much what it looks like.

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:12.800
Tim Ferriss: It’s kind of a Spiderman position.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:15.160
Jason Nemer: Yeah, Spiderman, upside down
monkey business.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:18.290
Tim Ferriss: Upside down with your feet together
sort of in a butterfly position.

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:19.290
Jason Nemer: Exactly.

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:25.420
Tim Ferriss: And then the person who’s supporting
you, you the base, are on your back, legs

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:29.150
roughly straight up in the air in an L-base
position.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:33.250
And your feet would be basically right on
top of my upper thighs.

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:34.250
Jason Nemer: Correct.

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:36.220
Tim Ferriss: Those are the shelves.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:37.959
I remember being spun around.

00:02:37.959 --> 00:02:41.950
I felt like I was in a washing machine of
sorts, which is actually a term of course

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:44.230
you use in Acro.

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:46.530
It just blew my mind.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.780
What are the origins of AcroYoga?

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:52.200
How did it come to be?

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:56.810
Jason Nemer: Any time we talk about origins,
there’s my version of my experience of the

00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:57.810
story.

00:02:57.810 --> 00:02:59.040
There’s the historical perspective.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:05.280
So what I’ll do is I’ll share from my
perspective what and how this all came together.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:08.290
Basically, I had a lot of acrobatic experience.

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:12.720
I started when I was 12 and I competed in
sports acrobatics, which is basically like

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.580
gymnastics and figure skating put together.

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:18.840
It’s a routine with partners, music, dance.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:20.580
So I competed in that for many years.

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:27.180
I found yoga in college and then ended up
in San Francisco, where I met Jenny, the co-founder.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:31.489
Jenny had a background in the therapeutic
flying so what you were describing, being

00:03:31.489 --> 00:03:35.270
upside down and being massaged, that’s something
that I learned from Jenny; she learned it

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:36.600
from other people.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:42.610
So AcroYoga to me was me and Jenny meeting
and pretty much putting together our bag of

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:43.610
tricks.

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:50.250
Tim Ferriss: That bag of tricks, I will say
just in terms of personal experience, has

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:56.879
been incredible because you have not just
the acrobatic – and this is a term that

00:03:56.879 --> 00:04:05.409
maybe you would not use, I don’t know, but
very kind of yang-driven strength, athleticism

00:04:05.409 --> 00:04:07.200
component which is the acrobatics.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:13.700
But then you have the very yin, very restorative
therapeutic Thai massage.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:15.659
There’s more to it, of course.

00:04:15.659 --> 00:04:21.579
But my hips, after now doing this reasonably
consistently, particularly the last two months

00:04:21.579 --> 00:04:24.180
but for the last however long since we met.

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:25.660
Jason Nemer: A year, year-plus, yeah.

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:26.889
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, a year-plus.

00:04:26.889 --> 00:04:27.889
Good lord.

00:04:27.889 --> 00:04:32.350
I realized today that I’ve been a professional
writer for ten years, almost to the dot.

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:33.350
Jason Nemer: Wow.

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:34.350
Well done.

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:35.710
Tim Ferriss: I was like, man that explains
the hair loss.

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:37.850
It’s been a long fucking time.

00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:49.410
But my hips, my knee issues, ankle flexibility,
my progress from grumpy baby position, for

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:51.460
those of you who know yoga at all.

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:54.889
Imagine you’re lying on your back kind of
holding onto your big toes and pulling your

00:04:54.889 --> 00:04:58.510
knees into your armpits.

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:04.449
Historically very uncomfortable for me; still
not my favorite place to be but much improved.

00:05:04.449 --> 00:05:07.470
And I feel years younger.

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:12.010
Athletically speaking, more mobile than I
was even when I was competing in wrestling

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:17.410
and in jiu-jitsu judo and whatnot.

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:21.180
What was the birth moment of AcroYoga, per
se?

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:26.830
When did that meeting turn into Acro, which
is now practiced in how many countries, would

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:27.830
you say?

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:29.889
Jason Nemer: I went up and counted recently.

00:05:29.889 --> 00:05:34.330
We have 55 countries that there are certified
teachers, but it doesn’t mean that the reach

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:35.430
isn’t bigger than that.

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:38.840
But it’s definitely a global practice at
this point.

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:41.860
The creation story is actually pretty magical.

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:47.120
Jenny and I had been hearing about each other
and we were training at the same circus center.

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:50.650
She was doing contortion during the day; I
was teaching trampoline at night.

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:54.070
So we kept orbiting around each other and
had a lot of common friends but just didn’t

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:55.070
meet.

00:05:55.070 --> 00:05:59.820
When we finally met, we got together at a
party and I immediately put her into a hand-to-hand

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:02.750
where she’s doing a handstand on my hands.

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:07.020
Then she put me in folded leaf, which is you
would say the down dog of therapeutic flying.

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:11.500
It’s where my body is in a hanging, surrendered
position.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:14.270
My head’s in her belly and she’s supporting
my weight.

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:19.600
That was the first time as an acrobat that
I felt what it was like to be inverted but

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:20.789
not be engaged.

00:06:20.789 --> 00:06:24.510
Because acrobats are very controlling and
controlled in their body.

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:29.289
But to surrender, it was amazing, just those
two poses.

00:06:29.289 --> 00:06:33.889
Then we were up until 5:00 a.m. talking about
this practice that would use all these different

00:06:33.889 --> 00:06:34.889
skill sets.

00:06:34.889 --> 00:06:39.190
We saw how things like partner yoga would
help people communicate and with the basic

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:44.319
therapeutic flying that’s not very advanced,
teaches people acrobatics in a soft way.

00:06:44.319 --> 00:06:47.710
We put really all the pieces together that
night.

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:53.389
Tim Ferriss: It was orbiting, orbiting, orbiting
and then –

00:06:53.389 --> 00:06:54.849
Jason Nemer: Collision.

00:06:54.849 --> 00:06:57.280
Tim Ferriss: Collision and synthesis.

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:01.831
Let’s rewind just for a second, to go back
to the – and I want to get the term right

00:07:01.831 --> 00:07:02.831
– the acrobatics.

00:07:02.831 --> 00:07:03.831
What was it?

00:07:03.831 --> 00:07:04.831
Jason Nemer: Sports acrobatics.

00:07:04.831 --> 00:07:07.740
Tim Ferriss: Sports acrobatics, which I believe
is also what Andrei Bondarenko uses.

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:10.069
Jason Nemer: Yes, he was a sports acrobat.

00:07:10.069 --> 00:07:13.720
Tim Ferriss: From Cirque du Soleil, who I
spent a bit of time with in Los Angeles when

00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:16.160
he was there, also did.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:17.270
What are the categories?

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:18.800
Because you have what?

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:23.580
It’s like two men, two women?

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:24.580
And then mixed gender?

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:25.580
How does it work?

00:07:25.580 --> 00:07:27.530
Jason Nemer: It’s the pairs; you have three
different pairs.

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:29.349
Men’s pair, women’s pair, mixed pair.

00:07:29.349 --> 00:07:31.720
You have women’s trio, and you have men’s
four.

00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:33.121
That’s the partner acrobatics.

00:07:33.121 --> 00:07:36.940
And parts of sports acrobatics is also platform
tumbling.

00:07:36.940 --> 00:07:41.720
This is when you have a 100-foot long strip
and people run full speed and do crazy flipping

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:42.720
and twisting.

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:43.720
Tim Ferriss: Right.

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:46.590
I saw, I think it was a quadruple back flip
from Russia.

00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:47.590
Jason Nemer: It was on my feed.

00:07:47.590 --> 00:07:48.590
Did you see it?

00:07:48.590 --> 00:07:50.789
Tim Ferriss: I think it was probably from
your feed or Coach Sommer’s feed.

00:07:50.789 --> 00:07:54.620
Jason Nemer: I’d never seen a quad tumbled;
I’d never even imagined it was possible.

00:07:54.620 --> 00:07:55.620
He did it easily.

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:56.620
It was nuts.

00:07:56.620 --> 00:07:57.729
Tim Ferriss: It was insane.

00:07:57.729 --> 00:08:01.500
So the men’s four, let’s talk about that
because I think this is something people may

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:09.139
have seen in performances in Vegas or elsewhere,
very often by people from Eastern Europe or

00:08:09.139 --> 00:08:10.790
former Soviet Union.

00:08:10.790 --> 00:08:11.790
What does that involve?

00:08:11.790 --> 00:08:15.200
Jason Nemer: Men’s four, you usually have
two bases that are about the same height,

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:20.979
same build; a middle which I would be a good
size for a middle in a men’s four.

00:08:20.979 --> 00:08:22.030
I’m 5’9”, 160.

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:25.360
And then the flyer, vary in sizes; always
the smallest one.

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:31.200
And basically if you know cheerleading basket
tosses, they do a lot of tempo skills, is

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:35.710
what it’s called, where the flyer does flips
and twists and lands back on the basket.

00:08:35.710 --> 00:08:38.340
There are actually three different routines.

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:41.300
There’s a balance, a tempo and a combined.

00:08:41.300 --> 00:08:44.430
Balanced routines in men’s four is just
a pyramid.

00:08:44.430 --> 00:08:49.600
It’s no music, and it can be the base is
doing a back bend, for you yogis – Urdhva

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:50.959
Dhanurasana.

00:08:50.959 --> 00:08:54.740
Then the three people are standing on this
guy’s belly, and the guy on the top is doing

00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:56.520
a one-armed hand stand, cray-cray.

00:08:56.520 --> 00:08:58.560
Tim Ferriss: That would have been Andrei?

00:08:58.560 --> 00:08:59.560
He was a flyer.

00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:01.839
Jason Nemer: Yeah, he was a flyer.

00:09:01.839 --> 00:09:05.170
I don’t know if he did men’s four or men’s
pair; I don’t remember.

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:07.790
Tim Ferriss: I’m almost positive he did
men’s four.

00:09:07.790 --> 00:09:08.820
He could correct us, of course.

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:09.820
Andrei Bondarenko.

00:09:09.820 --> 00:09:10.820
You should check him out on Instagram.

00:09:10.820 --> 00:09:12.060
Jason Nemer: Such a nice guy.

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:14.930
Tim Ferriss: Sweetheart of a guy, and just
a monster.

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:16.890
Jason Nemer: A badass.

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:17.890
Certified badass.

00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:23.130
Tim Ferriss: For those people who, as I didn’t
know, basket case?

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:24.579
No, not basket case.

00:09:24.579 --> 00:09:25.959
Jason Nemer: Basket toss.

00:09:25.959 --> 00:09:27.330
Tim Ferriss: Basket toss.

00:09:27.330 --> 00:09:28.570
Basket case is me.

00:09:28.570 --> 00:09:32.220
Basket toss sounds like something pornographic,
and it might be.

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:33.839
But in this case it’s effectively –

00:09:33.839 --> 00:09:36.880
Jason Nemer: Further down you hold your wrist.

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:42.250
Tim Ferriss: You hold your wrist and you’re
basically with other people weaving a –

00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:43.700
Jason Nemer: Human trampoline.

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:47.850
Tim Ferriss: – human trampoline with your
forearms.

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:51.970
It just blew my mind when I first saw, at
Cirque du Soleil.

00:09:51.970 --> 00:09:56.630
You were there when with Andrei we went behind
the scenes and we saw them warming up.

00:09:56.630 --> 00:10:03.240
They’re just getting launched 20, 25 feet
and then landing on this human trampoline

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:07.050
that is effectively the size of the top of
a stool.

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:08.560
It’s just incredible.

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:13.080
And I remember Andrei and I were having tea
and he was talking about at some point – and

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:16.830
I’m going to paraphrase this, obviously,
and maybe butcher it.

00:10:16.830 --> 00:10:21.350
In the Ukraine when he went to some type of
regional championship and there were a bunch

00:10:21.350 --> 00:10:24.070
of people warming up, different teams.

00:10:24.070 --> 00:10:28.350
One of the teams had a flyer who’s super
aggressive and wanted to make an impression

00:10:28.350 --> 00:10:32.830
on everyone and tried something really outrageously
hard.

00:10:32.830 --> 00:10:39.579
It was a concrete floor and he just took a
head-first spill on the concrete floor.

00:10:39.579 --> 00:10:42.420
Those folks got carted out and the competition
continued.

00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:51.620
That was his path, all of their paths, out
of that world and into one of prosperity and

00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:54.440
economic feasibility for careers.

00:10:54.440 --> 00:10:58.620
Jason Nemer: I heard a very similar story,
and this is something that I see as a potential

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:00.860
pitfall of acrobatics and potential pitfall.

00:11:00.860 --> 00:11:04.529
I trained in Bulgaria when I was 15 with the
Bulgarian national team.

00:11:04.529 --> 00:11:08.180
The year before we brought them to California
and took them to Disneyland – blew their

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:10.389
minds – and learned from them.

00:11:10.389 --> 00:11:14.830
I heard a few years after that that the men’s
four, same thing; wanted to intimidate the

00:11:14.830 --> 00:11:15.830
other men’s four.

00:11:15.830 --> 00:11:17.800
So what I was describing are these four high
pyramids.

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:23.560
They were doing a four high, one-armed pyramid
and they fell and the kid broke most of his

00:11:23.560 --> 00:11:24.560
bones.

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:27.899
He didn’t die but it was the end of his
career.

00:11:27.899 --> 00:11:33.260
I think also for the 2008 Olympics in Beijing,
there were a number of Chinese gymnasts who

00:11:33.260 --> 00:11:37.520
died trying vaults; trying to do these very
difficult vaults.

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:42.580
Yeah, the dark side of acrobatics can be when
your body is an obstacle to your goal, and

00:11:42.580 --> 00:11:47.519
this is where the yoga and acrobatics can
weave so intelligently the dynamic power of

00:11:47.519 --> 00:11:51.779
acrobatics but the reality that you’ve got
in your body for your whole life.

00:11:51.779 --> 00:11:55.649
How do you make these educated steps towards
what you want?

00:11:55.649 --> 00:12:02.500
Tim Ferriss: This comes back to the surrender
component, which may sound very woo-woo to

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:06.700
some people listening and that’s okay because
we’re in San Francisco, so suck it up and

00:12:06.700 --> 00:12:07.700
take it.

00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:08.700
Jason Nemer: Woo-woo!

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:12.250
Tim Ferriss: We’re not going to get too
crazy.

00:12:12.250 --> 00:12:14.290
Maybe we will.

00:12:14.290 --> 00:12:17.290
I’m flashing all over the place; I know
this is a bit like Memento.

00:12:17.290 --> 00:12:23.860
But one of my other influences is this coach
named Jerzy Gregorek, who is the Polish Olympic

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:26.839
lifting coach I’ve mentioned a few times.

00:12:26.839 --> 00:12:28.459
He’s 62.

00:12:28.459 --> 00:12:35.720
Leaner, stronger, more mobile than I am by
many factors and he’s probably 135, 140.

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:43.740
He can get on an Indo Board, like a balance
board and hit a perfect snatch with a barbell

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:51.250
loaded full of weight, ass to the floor and
then stand up and put it back on the floor

00:12:51.250 --> 00:12:53.260
with effectively no warm-up, like cold.

00:12:53.260 --> 00:12:55.540
He’s just an extremely impressive athlete.

00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:58.570
His longevity is phenomenal.

00:12:58.570 --> 00:13:01.810
He said to me at one point – because my
habit, of course, is push, push, push, break.

00:13:01.810 --> 00:13:02.810
Jason Nemer: You?

00:13:02.810 --> 00:13:03.810
Push?

00:13:03.810 --> 00:13:04.810
Tim Ferriss: I know.

00:13:04.810 --> 00:13:06.870
Stop everything for a while because I’m
incapacitated.

00:13:06.870 --> 00:13:08.399
Push, push, push, break.

00:13:08.399 --> 00:13:11.530
And he said, “You have a Ferrari engine.”

00:13:11.530 --> 00:13:12.550
I’m paraphrasing here.

00:13:12.550 --> 00:13:16.899
But he said, “Remember, you have a Ferrari
engine in a Hyundai body.

00:13:16.899 --> 00:13:21.250
Like you’re going to just blow yourself
apart, or it’s like your mind is willing

00:13:21.250 --> 00:13:23.360
but your body is not adapted.”

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:28.171
So you need to – not his words, mine – chill
the fuck out and play the long game, which

00:13:28.171 --> 00:13:31.690
I’m becoming better at.

00:13:31.690 --> 00:13:36.660
Part of what has aided that is the therapeutics.

00:13:36.660 --> 00:13:39.710
Something as simple as you mentioned surrender.

00:13:39.710 --> 00:13:45.760
So in the type of training I’m focusing
on right now, I’m actually doing the GymnasticBodies

00:13:45.760 --> 00:13:50.949
work with Coach Summer, he’s the former
national team coach for men’s in the U.S.

00:13:50.949 --> 00:13:52.740
He was on the podcast, as well.

00:13:52.740 --> 00:13:57.850
But that is not a surrender type of training,
and it can’t be to do it properly.

00:13:57.850 --> 00:13:59.980
And I really, really enjoy it.

00:13:59.980 --> 00:14:03.790
But if I’m doing that, say, two days of
stretching, two days of strength training

00:14:03.790 --> 00:14:09.470
per week, I might then be doing – and I
have been doing – Acro on Mondays, Wednesdays

00:14:09.470 --> 00:14:15.720
and Fridays for an hour to an hour and a half
in the mornings.

00:14:15.720 --> 00:14:22.139
Just the traction alone that you’re able
to get, and for those people who are wondering

00:14:22.139 --> 00:14:26.080
what the hell traction is, simply put – and
chiropractors may disagree with this – but

00:14:26.080 --> 00:14:31.339
let’s just imagine you’re on an inversion
table and you get flipped to a point where

00:14:31.339 --> 00:14:35.350
your head is below your feet and you feel
your spine is being decompressed; think of

00:14:35.350 --> 00:14:36.600
that as one form of traction.

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:40.949
You can get traction on your wrists, traction
on your ankles.

00:14:40.949 --> 00:14:45.070
I thought what might be fun, and this is going
to be a visual component; we’ll try to describe

00:14:45.070 --> 00:14:48.190
it, but you mentioned a couple of things.

00:14:48.190 --> 00:14:51.540
You mentioned folded leaf.

00:14:51.540 --> 00:14:54.790
Would you say that is a good place for people
to start with Acro?

00:14:54.790 --> 00:14:58.250
Jason Nemer: There are a lot of good places
to start.

00:14:58.250 --> 00:15:02.330
Therapeutic flying, what I like about it is
it’s very soft and gentle.

00:15:02.330 --> 00:15:06.890
If there’s extreme mobility issues, I think
there are other things people can do to gain

00:15:06.890 --> 00:15:10.180
confidence and to learn how to communicate
what they need.

00:15:10.180 --> 00:15:13.220
The way that we teach the practice is base/flyer/spotter.

00:15:13.220 --> 00:15:17.570
So the spotter cannot only keep it safe but
they can also make sure that the base is in

00:15:17.570 --> 00:15:18.570
the correct alignment.

00:15:18.570 --> 00:15:22.920
So it’s too hard to say that this pose is
the exact place to start.

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:23.920
Tim Ferriss: But it’s one place to start.

00:15:23.920 --> 00:15:25.180
Jason Nemer: It is a good place to start.

00:15:25.180 --> 00:15:28.721
Tim Ferriss: Now, when you say base/flyer/spotter
– that is simply to say that generally you’re

00:15:28.721 --> 00:15:29.779
practicing in groups of three?

00:15:29.779 --> 00:15:31.180
Jason Nemer: Yes.

00:15:31.180 --> 00:15:36.971
Tim Ferriss: Let’s just show, and for those
people who are listening to audio, I’m sure

00:15:36.971 --> 00:15:40.300
I mentioned it in the intro and I’ll probably
mention it at the outro.

00:15:40.300 --> 00:15:45.070
If not, you can check in the show notes, fourhourworkweek.com/podcast,
but we’re capturing this on video.

00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:48.790
So you might as well do some stuff that’s
well suited to video, and we’ll talk through

00:15:48.790 --> 00:15:49.949
it.

00:15:49.949 --> 00:15:52.530
Let’s go to this mat, here.

00:15:52.530 --> 00:15:54.130
Jason Nemer: Oh, wait.

00:15:54.130 --> 00:15:55.519
We’ve got to do it the right way.

00:15:55.519 --> 00:15:57.399
Tim Ferriss: I was going to catch you!

00:15:57.399 --> 00:15:59.610
Jason Nemer: My Dutch friends would kill me.

00:15:59.610 --> 00:16:04.080
Tim Ferriss: Then I lean back, and then we
bring the partner down.

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:07.000
So apparently the Dutch acrobats get very
upset if you don’t do that.

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:09.009
And I’ve gotten to the point where I’m
like, you know what?

00:16:09.009 --> 00:16:10.300
I’ve had enough of the Dutch.

00:16:10.300 --> 00:16:13.620
I love their kickboxing, Ernesto Hoost, love
you.

00:16:13.620 --> 00:16:17.340
But from all the jiu-jitsu I’m just like,
flop.

00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:20.370
I’ve made a lot of – I’m not going to
say enemies; that doesn’t really happen

00:16:20.370 --> 00:16:21.370
in Acro.

00:16:21.370 --> 00:16:25.730
So I am standing with my feet reasonably close
to your hips.

00:16:25.730 --> 00:16:28.490
Jason Nemer: I want to be able to touch my
flyer’s feet.

00:16:28.490 --> 00:16:30.630
My feet are turned out just below the hip
bones.

00:16:30.630 --> 00:16:32.529
Tim Ferriss: I am standing straight up.

00:16:32.529 --> 00:16:35.070
It’s almost as if you’ve seen the UFC.

00:16:35.070 --> 00:16:37.230
You’re like open guard on your back.

00:16:37.230 --> 00:16:39.730
This would be where I try to punch you and
then you’d kick me in the face but that’s

00:16:39.730 --> 00:16:41.519
not Acro, that’s MMA.

00:16:41.519 --> 00:16:42.519
All right.

00:16:42.519 --> 00:16:44.170
And then flyer, fingers forward.

00:16:44.170 --> 00:16:45.170
Jason Nemer: You’ve got it.

00:16:45.170 --> 00:16:46.170
You’ve got the acronyms and everything.

00:16:46.170 --> 00:16:50.200
Tim Ferriss: I’m a young Paduan just learning
what I can.

00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:54.769
Jason Nemer: The most important thing is that
you understand when to be water and when to

00:16:54.769 --> 00:16:56.380
be earth, and what parts of your body.

00:16:56.380 --> 00:16:59.690
So I’m going to be watery with my arms and
really sturdy with my legs.

00:16:59.690 --> 00:17:01.329
So we’ll take a deep breath.

00:17:01.329 --> 00:17:05.690
On the exhale, I receive with everything and
then I push up with the legs.

00:17:05.690 --> 00:17:09.370
Here, a lot of times the flyer will want to
put the hands down and practice their handstand

00:17:09.370 --> 00:17:10.919
training; that means they’re a control freak.

00:17:10.919 --> 00:17:16.150
Tim Ferriss: So you fold the top of their
hands down to the floor.

00:17:16.150 --> 00:17:21.600
So at this point, for those people listening,
I am supported on Jason’s feet.

00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:23.480
I’m completely upside down.

00:17:23.480 --> 00:17:32.049
I’m basically in an L-straddle position,
for those of you who know anything about gymnastics.

00:17:32.049 --> 00:17:35.140
So this is folded leaf.

00:17:35.140 --> 00:17:39.370
Sometimes in hush tones, jokingly referred
to as leaf blower.

00:17:39.370 --> 00:17:41.260
Jason Nemer: Oh, my God.

00:17:41.260 --> 00:17:43.515
I didn’t know that was going to come out
in the podcast.

00:17:43.515 --> 00:17:45.320
Tim Ferriss: I’m trying to help people with
the images, here.

00:17:45.320 --> 00:17:46.539
This is scarecrow, right?

00:17:46.539 --> 00:17:48.550
Jason Nemer: This is scarecrow.

00:17:48.550 --> 00:17:52.679
Basically what I do as a base is I try to
let your body unwind patterns.

00:17:52.679 --> 00:17:57.930
So as I bounce and shake, a lot of times our
mind is connected to muscle groups and flexing

00:17:57.930 --> 00:17:59.809
them when they don’t need to be.

00:17:59.809 --> 00:18:06.280
So the true therapist is gravity, which helps
to let your spine hang like a plumb line.

00:18:06.280 --> 00:18:13.690
Tim Ferriss: Could we show, for instance,
super yogi?

00:18:13.690 --> 00:18:15.220
Jason Nemer: Sure.

00:18:15.220 --> 00:18:17.500
Tim likes traction.

00:18:17.500 --> 00:18:20.909
So what I can do is I can let my legs come
off my 90 degree.

00:18:20.909 --> 00:18:22.010
His weight will start falling back.

00:18:22.010 --> 00:18:23.270
We’ll take a deep breath together.

00:18:23.270 --> 00:18:28.380
On the exhale, my legs go back and I resist
with the arms.

00:18:28.380 --> 00:18:29.380
Okay?

00:18:29.380 --> 00:18:30.380
Two more.

00:18:30.380 --> 00:18:31.380
Inhale, exhale.

00:18:31.380 --> 00:18:34.690
So I’m pushing the legs back then giving
resistance.

00:18:34.690 --> 00:18:37.550
You’ve got a little wrist traction there.

00:18:37.550 --> 00:18:43.840
One more time, inhale and exhale.

00:18:43.840 --> 00:18:47.870
And then where I love to go is to open up
your triceps and your shoulders.

00:18:47.870 --> 00:18:50.910
So I’m going to place my hands under his
elbows and he can bend the elbows and the

00:18:50.910 --> 00:18:52.070
knees at the same rate.

00:18:52.070 --> 00:18:53.580
Okay, bring the elbows closer.

00:18:53.580 --> 00:18:55.680
Does that feel okay on your shoulder?

00:18:55.680 --> 00:18:59.890
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I’m going to be a little
careful on the left just because I had it

00:18:59.890 --> 00:19:01.669
reconstructed but yeah, this feels fine.

00:19:01.669 --> 00:19:05.120
Jason Nemer: And in general where the body
can be vulnerable in the flying is in the

00:19:05.120 --> 00:19:08.700
shoulders and the lower back so communication
is really important, and just listening to

00:19:08.700 --> 00:19:09.700
your body.

00:19:09.700 --> 00:19:10.700
All good?

00:19:10.700 --> 00:19:12.480
Tim Ferriss: All good.

00:19:12.480 --> 00:19:15.180
Jason Nemer: Sweet.

00:19:15.180 --> 00:19:20.130
Side bending is also a thing that can really
help the health of the lower back.

00:19:20.130 --> 00:19:23.909
And what’s different with the therapeutic
flying from yoga is your body doesn’t have

00:19:23.909 --> 00:19:24.909
to be engaged.

00:19:24.909 --> 00:19:30.760
You’re not using your muscles as the flyer
so you don’t have to work uphill, basically.

00:19:30.760 --> 00:19:32.980
Yeah, mon.

00:19:32.980 --> 00:19:34.650
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, mon.

00:19:34.650 --> 00:19:39.330
So those are some basics of therapeutic flying.

00:19:39.330 --> 00:19:45.140
Some people who heard a bunch of grunting
and weird noises and just don’t know what

00:19:45.140 --> 00:19:48.700
the hell is happening, or from people who
might have seen the video.

00:19:48.700 --> 00:19:51.340
Actually, let’s demonstrate one more thing.

00:19:51.340 --> 00:19:58.320
Because I was in South America doing an immersive,
which was a great experience, meaning a two-week

00:19:58.320 --> 00:20:01.230
intensive of AcroYoga training.

00:20:01.230 --> 00:20:07.500
The first week was dedicated to the acrobatics,
second week to the therapeutic flying and

00:20:07.500 --> 00:20:09.520
Thai massage.

00:20:09.520 --> 00:20:15.151
I was visiting a friend of mine named Chris
who has developed some lower back and hip

00:20:15.151 --> 00:20:17.260
issues from a lot of sitting.

00:20:17.260 --> 00:20:20.159
He’s a former high-level athlete.

00:20:20.159 --> 00:20:28.760
I gave him what is sometimes referred to in
the Acro community as leg love, which is typically

00:20:28.760 --> 00:20:31.770
done – for those of you who just saw it
or I’ll just describe it.

00:20:31.770 --> 00:20:36.710
So Jason is on his back basing me, supporting
all of my weight on his legs, in effect.

00:20:36.710 --> 00:20:39.860
That can get tiring.

00:20:39.860 --> 00:20:45.140
And so after you’ve received effectively
massage, being floated on someone’s feet,

00:20:45.140 --> 00:20:46.680
you then help to –

00:20:46.680 --> 00:20:48.800
Jason Nemer: Release the tension.

00:20:48.800 --> 00:20:51.640
Tim Ferriss: – release the tension in their
legs and restore their legs a bit.

00:20:51.640 --> 00:20:52.640
Jason Nemer: Exactly.

00:20:52.640 --> 00:20:53.640
Tim Ferriss: And so I did that.

00:20:53.640 --> 00:20:54.710
Actually, I’ll demonstrate.

00:20:54.710 --> 00:20:56.250
Jason Nemer: All right.

00:20:56.250 --> 00:20:57.250
Bring it.

00:20:57.250 --> 00:20:58.990
This podcast is getting better and better.

00:20:58.990 --> 00:21:05.150
Tim Ferriss: So what I did for Chris, and
this is something I’ve done for a number

00:21:05.150 --> 00:21:06.150
of people, now.

00:21:06.150 --> 00:21:08.940
And obviously, consult your qualified AcroYoga
professional.

00:21:08.940 --> 00:21:11.970
I’m no doctor; don’t play one on the internet.

00:21:11.970 --> 00:21:14.789
But let’s say we just came down.

00:21:14.789 --> 00:21:17.230
So boom, we came down and we’re here.

00:21:17.230 --> 00:21:19.559
There are a couple of very easy things you
can do.

00:21:19.559 --> 00:21:23.440
So just from this squat position, I’ll get
his legs straight.

00:21:23.440 --> 00:21:30.750
Then I’ll actually just hold his feet in
between the crease of my leg and my upper

00:21:30.750 --> 00:21:32.870
body and then lean backwards.

00:21:32.870 --> 00:21:37.700
So I’m giving him traction at the hip and
the lower back, even though I’m not holding

00:21:37.700 --> 00:21:38.700
onto anything.

00:21:38.700 --> 00:21:39.850
Jason Nemer: It’s so good on my ankles,
too.

00:21:39.850 --> 00:21:44.980
Tim Ferriss: And a lot of traction on the
ankles and external rotation.

00:21:44.980 --> 00:21:51.090
Other things that are pretty easy to do would
include, you can do a little bit more traction.

00:21:51.090 --> 00:21:56.020
So you step in, and I’m just going to shake
the legs to get them to relax a bit, and then

00:21:56.020 --> 00:22:00.210
turn the feet in, put them behind my hips
and lean back.

00:22:00.210 --> 00:22:05.760
Jason Nemer: What’s great about this pose
is therapeutic flying is an external rotation;

00:22:05.760 --> 00:22:08.110
this is a counter pose, it’s an internal
rotation.

00:22:08.110 --> 00:22:12.510
So that’s where some of the yoga intelligence
starts sneaking in, is how do you counter

00:22:12.510 --> 00:22:13.880
the muscles you’ve been working?

00:22:13.880 --> 00:22:18.150
Tim Ferriss: And for those people who are
say, like cross fitters who are afraid of

00:22:18.150 --> 00:22:23.620
seeming too crunchy to their buddies or whatever
it might be, this is an excellent complement

00:22:23.620 --> 00:22:27.390
to any type of Olympic lifting or lifting
that involves squatting.

00:22:27.390 --> 00:22:32.900
I’ve found this to just be a huge performance
enhancer, to have this done.

00:22:32.900 --> 00:22:37.630
Jason Nemer: Can you just get on my feet for
one second in a bird, just to show what my

00:22:37.630 --> 00:22:43.549
theory is of why your hips are a lot more
evolved now?

00:22:43.549 --> 00:22:44.549
Squatting flexibility.

00:22:44.549 --> 00:22:45.549
Tim Ferriss: Look at this guy’s hips.

00:22:45.549 --> 00:22:50.240
Jason Nemer: It gets increased when you have
170, 180 pounds on top of you.

00:22:50.240 --> 00:22:54.640
So it doesn’t just warm the body but it
lets you access new flexibility.

00:22:54.640 --> 00:22:57.650
Tim Ferriss: That’s one that you need to
watch the video for.

00:22:57.650 --> 00:22:59.850
Yeah, if you want to see freakish hip mobility.

00:22:59.850 --> 00:23:01.480
Jason Nemer: I have open hips; it’s true.

00:23:01.480 --> 00:23:05.290
Tim Ferriss: Open hips, good birthing hips;
then you should watch the video.

00:23:05.290 --> 00:23:09.900
I’ll just demonstrate a few more that are
super simple.

00:23:09.900 --> 00:23:11.659
There are many, many options.

00:23:11.659 --> 00:23:17.659
We could do bus driver; that’s what this
one is known as.

00:23:17.659 --> 00:23:23.650
They all look really silly and they are as
profound, I think, for athletic performance

00:23:23.650 --> 00:23:27.980
or just general health, as they are, in some
cases, ridiculous looking.

00:23:27.980 --> 00:23:34.539
Another that I’ve found just incredibly
helpful is just swinging.

00:23:34.539 --> 00:23:36.770
So this type of movement.

00:23:36.770 --> 00:23:46.340
I won’t do too many more; you can get fancy
and do like the circle eights and stuff but

00:23:46.340 --> 00:23:50.850
I’ll show one more and then we’ll get
back to the interview.

00:23:50.850 --> 00:23:56.620
So in this position, and feel free to correct
me if I’m messing this up, but you can effectively

00:23:56.620 --> 00:24:02.500
push down on the feet and get the hips off
the ground, and then use the hand – or not

00:24:02.500 --> 00:24:09.580
– to loosen up this entire lower leg and
hip region.

00:24:09.580 --> 00:24:21.900
Literally just doing those few things, I was
able to alleviate Chris’s lower back pain

00:24:21.900 --> 00:24:25.350
significantly, symptoms 40 to 50 percent decreased.

00:24:25.350 --> 00:24:26.520
Jason Nemer: Awesome.

00:24:26.520 --> 00:24:31.529
Tim Ferriss: That is, in my experience, not
just a Band-Aid.

00:24:31.529 --> 00:24:34.370
It’s not like you swallowed a bunch of ibuprofen.

00:24:34.370 --> 00:24:38.520
With regular use, the effect seems to be very
persistent.

00:24:38.520 --> 00:24:42.950
My hips feel great and it’s like I never
thought in my entire life I would ever say

00:24:42.950 --> 00:24:43.950
that.

00:24:43.950 --> 00:24:44.950
Jason Nemer: That makes me so happy.

00:24:44.950 --> 00:24:49.610
That is the goal of the practice is that you
can find a way to interact with AcroYoga that

00:24:49.610 --> 00:24:53.059
will affirm who you are now and who you’re
going to be for the rest of your life.

00:24:53.059 --> 00:24:59.520
One of the really cool things that I’ve
seen is acrobats, yogis and healers, all three

00:24:59.520 --> 00:25:03.360
lineages, people can do it until they’re
90, 100.

00:25:03.360 --> 00:25:04.360
Gymnasts, not true.

00:25:04.360 --> 00:25:08.539
I have not seen 100-year-old, high-level gymnasts,
just because it’s very high impact.

00:25:08.539 --> 00:25:13.070
But there are styles of acrobatics, styles
of yoga and styles of healing that are really

00:25:13.070 --> 00:25:17.159
affirming who you are so you’re not depleting
yourself as you’re learning these cool things.

00:25:17.159 --> 00:25:22.860
Tim Ferriss: Coach Summers said, I think it
was, I know in the world of high level gymnastics,

00:25:22.860 --> 00:25:26.010
I know a lot of stupid people and I know a
lot of old people.

00:25:26.010 --> 00:25:30.350
I don’t know a lot of stupid, old people
who have made it that long.

00:25:30.350 --> 00:25:37.100
But speaking of some older folks, you’ve
mentioned to me a number of your teachers,

00:25:37.100 --> 00:25:40.549
a number of your influences.

00:25:40.549 --> 00:25:45.400
Could you talk about Lu Yi and your experience
with your healing teacher, and just give some

00:25:45.400 --> 00:25:46.649
context on who these people are.

00:25:46.649 --> 00:25:47.649
Jason Nemer: Sure.

00:25:47.649 --> 00:25:52.350
So Lu Yi is a Chinese acrobatic master; he’s
a circus master.

00:25:52.350 --> 00:25:54.950
He traveled throughout the world back in the
‘50s.

00:25:54.950 --> 00:25:59.960
He was actually telling me a story about when
he performed in Africa and they had this act

00:25:59.960 --> 00:26:04.630
where you throw these big pots around and
the pots land on your head.

00:26:04.630 --> 00:26:07.240
And the Africans carry things on their heads
so they weren’t impressed; they didn’t

00:26:07.240 --> 00:26:08.580
clap and they had to take it out.

00:26:08.580 --> 00:26:13.210
So he’s just a treasure trove of really
amazing stories about the circus.

00:26:13.210 --> 00:26:16.620
He’s been in the Bay Area for quite some
time now.

00:26:16.620 --> 00:26:18.590
I met him about 13 years ago.

00:26:18.590 --> 00:26:20.679
I’ll give a little short story about this.

00:26:20.679 --> 00:26:22.929
I met him, I go into the circus center.

00:26:22.929 --> 00:26:26.520
I compete at high-level acrobatics but I hadn’t
trained high level in a while.

00:26:26.520 --> 00:26:28.799
I see him training people with one-armed handstands.

00:26:28.799 --> 00:26:32.549
And I as a base never learned how to do a
one-armed handstand on the ground by myself.

00:26:32.549 --> 00:26:36.820
So I’m like, “Mr. Lu Yi, you look like
such an amazing teacher.

00:26:36.820 --> 00:26:38.470
I’d be so honored to work with you.

00:26:38.470 --> 00:26:40.150
I just did a yoga teacher training.”

00:26:40.150 --> 00:26:42.049
And yoga is a four-letter word.

00:26:42.049 --> 00:26:44.650
I might as well have just spit in his face.

00:26:44.650 --> 00:26:48.539
So literally for the next three years, every
day I would see him in the circus center.

00:26:48.539 --> 00:26:50.130
“Mr. Lu Yi, I’d love to work with you.”

00:26:50.130 --> 00:26:52.040
“Oh, you have too much experience.

00:26:52.040 --> 00:26:54.270
Oh, you very old.

00:26:54.270 --> 00:26:55.950
My way different way.”

00:26:55.950 --> 00:26:59.840
When I speak with my pretend Chinese accent,
it’s in so much love.

00:26:59.840 --> 00:27:02.860
Tim Ferriss: It’s still pretty good, having
spent a lot of time in China.

00:27:02.860 --> 00:27:04.029
Jason Nemer: Oh, my God.

00:27:04.029 --> 00:27:10.190
So this is Lu Yi, and Lu Yi has been a mentor
in a lot of ways.

00:27:10.190 --> 00:27:11.820
He has a lot of different techniques.

00:27:11.820 --> 00:27:16.070
He’s right when he says that because he
came from circus and I came from sports acrobatics.

00:27:16.070 --> 00:27:20.649
One day I go into the circus center and he’s
teaching a kid how to ride on a bicycle backwards

00:27:20.649 --> 00:27:22.420
by sitting on the handlebars.

00:27:22.420 --> 00:27:25.830
He knows so many random things.

00:27:25.830 --> 00:27:31.690
And recently, just this year, he has sciatica
and he got into a car accident and it got

00:27:31.690 --> 00:27:34.559
worse, and he’s basically been out of commission
now for quite a while.

00:27:34.559 --> 00:27:39.390
I’ve been trying to connect him with one
of my other master teachers, who is Scott

00:27:39.390 --> 00:27:45.000
Blossom, who is an amazing Chinese medicine
doctor, acupuncture, Ayurveda, which is an

00:27:45.000 --> 00:27:48.860
Indian science of medicine, and he’s also
a shadow yoga teacher.

00:27:48.860 --> 00:27:53.029
And in the world I think there are 20 certified
shadow yoga instructors; that’s a whole

00:27:53.029 --> 00:27:57.440
other conversation but he’s basically a
triple threat, amazing human.

00:27:57.440 --> 00:28:01.710
Today before this interview, I went to Lu
Yi’s house.

00:28:01.710 --> 00:28:04.860
I picked him up, I took him over to Scott’s
office.

00:28:04.860 --> 00:28:12.179
Lu Yi doesn’t speak English very well, and
I understand him and we have a good rapport,

00:28:12.179 --> 00:28:13.570
so it was really good to be there.

00:28:13.570 --> 00:28:18.650
And for about six to eight minutes, he was
just reading his pulses, just feeling his

00:28:18.650 --> 00:28:21.120
hands.

00:28:21.120 --> 00:28:27.140
From there, he basically did needles, did
a lot of different techniques.

00:28:27.140 --> 00:28:31.809
Before he got him on the table, I was in a
moment where I just sat on the floor while

00:28:31.809 --> 00:28:32.809
they were doing the pulses.

00:28:32.809 --> 00:28:36.049
I closed my eyes and I did a mettā practice.

00:28:36.049 --> 00:28:39.150
Mettā means loving kindness and this comes
from Thai massage.

00:28:39.150 --> 00:28:43.399
Thai massage is an amazing practice that weaves
spirituality and healing.

00:28:43.399 --> 00:28:48.519
So as I close my eyes and I’m meditating,
my heart is pumping, my hands are getting

00:28:48.519 --> 00:28:52.200
hot; I’m basically cultivating all this
qi.

00:28:52.200 --> 00:28:55.840
And I didn’t think I was going to be able
to lay hands on Lu Yi at all, but then when

00:28:55.840 --> 00:28:58.190
he got him on the table, he’s like, “Jason
feel here.

00:28:58.190 --> 00:28:59.549
Okay, put an elbow there.”

00:28:59.549 --> 00:29:05.769
So I have two of my masters here and I got
to give love, basically, to this man who with

00:29:05.769 --> 00:29:09.649
his hands has sculpted my handstand, my one-arm
handstand.

00:29:09.649 --> 00:29:15.640
So it was just a really cool thing to feel
how acrobatics is really powerful but nothing

00:29:15.640 --> 00:29:18.620
is more important than your health until you
start losing your health.

00:29:18.620 --> 00:29:22.789
And to have these tools to help people feel
better, there are very few things in life

00:29:22.789 --> 00:29:27.470
that I think are more noble to dedicate to
than learning new things to help people.

00:29:27.470 --> 00:29:32.570
And mettā, loving kindness, compassion, meditation;
these are things that are very easy for everyone

00:29:32.570 --> 00:29:36.299
on the planet to start practicing as soon
as they build the desire.

00:29:36.299 --> 00:29:39.850
And I think human nature is such that we have
the desire that we want to help people, we

00:29:39.850 --> 00:29:41.750
want to make people feel better.

00:29:41.750 --> 00:29:46.540
So yeah, that was an amazing way to start
my day, to see these two masters come together.

00:29:46.540 --> 00:29:53.399
Tim Ferriss: I’ve been very struck by a
few things just related to what you just said.

00:29:53.399 --> 00:30:00.000
The first is that a lot of people who come
into healing have a history of being injured

00:29:53.399 --> 00:30:00.000
The first is that a lot of people who come
into healing have a history of being injured

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:02.730
themselves, or even injuring other people.

00:30:02.730 --> 00:30:10.510
A lot of Judoka in Japan end up then going
into bone setting or traditional, let’s

00:30:10.510 --> 00:30:14.860
just call it not traditional, but Western
medicine.

00:30:14.860 --> 00:30:19.919
The second piece is that I was very surprised
when I did the immersive.

00:30:19.919 --> 00:30:25.860
I hadn’t had any real in-depth exposure
to the therapeutic flying.

00:30:25.860 --> 00:30:31.360
That having been so aggressive in all of my
individual sports to date, I’ve always,

00:30:31.360 --> 00:30:38.710
since I was introduced to Acro, been obsessed
with the more dynamic, violent perhaps –

00:30:38.710 --> 00:30:41.340
Jason Nemer: The shiny objects.

00:30:41.340 --> 00:30:42.340
Squirrel!

00:30:42.340 --> 00:30:43.340
Tim Ferriss: The shiny objects.

00:30:43.340 --> 00:30:46.490
Yeah, which are – yeah, that’s the story
of my life.

00:30:46.490 --> 00:30:53.210
But I was astonished to find that when I did
the immersive, I was actually in many ways

00:30:53.210 --> 00:30:57.640
more drawn to the therapeutics.

00:30:57.640 --> 00:31:05.289
I think that’s because I’ve just over
developed or over focused on the hard driving

00:31:05.289 --> 00:31:08.750
aspect to such an extent that I don’t have
a rate limiter.

00:31:08.750 --> 00:31:12.539
It’s like on my tachometer, I don’t have
any red zone.

00:31:12.539 --> 00:31:15.470
I don’t even see it; it just blows apart.

00:31:15.470 --> 00:31:18.340
That helps me to start to calibrate and pay
more attention.

00:31:18.340 --> 00:31:25.100
By paying attention to someone else’s body,
I feel like you learn to pay attention and

00:31:25.100 --> 00:31:26.100
listen to your own body.

00:31:26.100 --> 00:31:28.840
Jason Nemer: For sure, especially if you have
good guidance.

00:31:28.840 --> 00:31:32.850
That is a very strong principle from a lot
of my teachers; body comfortable.

00:31:32.850 --> 00:31:37.160
If you’re not comfortable as a giver of
healing arts, you’re violating the first

00:31:37.160 --> 00:31:38.160
thing.

00:31:38.160 --> 00:31:41.179
So you actually get to be – and unfortunately
in English there’s not a good term for this

00:31:41.179 --> 00:31:42.929
– selfish.

00:31:42.929 --> 00:31:45.220
To be a good healer, you have to be very selfish.

00:31:45.220 --> 00:31:50.039
You have to really listen to who you are,
to where you are and from that place if you

00:31:50.039 --> 00:31:54.310
really understand, you have the potential
to give in a way that’s not depleting, give

00:31:54.310 --> 00:31:57.940
in a way that’s not going to hurt you because
that’s going to be a very short healing

00:31:57.940 --> 00:31:58.940
career.

00:31:58.940 --> 00:32:04.679
Tim Ferriss: What is the handstand approach
that you thought of this morning?

00:32:04.679 --> 00:32:07.690
So you mentioned this, and I said save it
for the podcast; I don’t want to hear about

00:32:07.690 --> 00:32:08.860
it.

00:32:08.860 --> 00:32:09.860
So tell us.

00:32:09.860 --> 00:32:11.370
A lot of people listening are interested in
handstands.

00:32:11.370 --> 00:32:13.340
There are a lot of crappy handstands out there.

00:32:13.340 --> 00:32:18.539
Jason Nemer: I’ve seen most of them in different
shapes and forms.

00:32:18.539 --> 00:32:22.300
This is where, again, I feel like the acrobatics
and the yoga start to mix.

00:32:22.300 --> 00:32:25.860
I’ve been doing shadow yoga for about six
years, now.

00:32:25.860 --> 00:32:27.340
There are three preludes [inaudible] –

00:32:27.340 --> 00:32:30.700
Tim Ferriss: Does Ra’s al Ghul – he’s
the bad guy in Batman – he doesn’t run

00:32:30.700 --> 00:32:31.700
shadow yoga.

00:32:31.700 --> 00:32:34.860
It sounds like something out of like an Avengers
movie.

00:32:34.860 --> 00:32:35.860
Jason Nemer: Marvel comics?

00:32:35.860 --> 00:32:36.860
Kind of.

00:32:36.860 --> 00:32:38.850
Zhander Remete is a Hungarian martial artist
who came up with this.

00:32:38.850 --> 00:32:40.510
Tim Ferriss: Sounds like Ra’s al Ghul.

00:32:40.510 --> 00:32:42.020
Jason Nemer: There’s some darkness to it.

00:32:42.020 --> 00:32:43.680
Tim Ferriss: We’ll come back to that.

00:32:43.680 --> 00:32:46.100
Okay, so we’ll dig into shadow but please
continue.

00:32:46.100 --> 00:32:48.760
Jason Nemer: It came from my shadow practice
this morning.

00:32:48.760 --> 00:32:52.019
So there’s a pose, and I’ll just do it
real quick.

00:32:52.019 --> 00:32:55.970
There’s a sequence in one of the poses is
with the fingers are laced.

00:32:55.970 --> 00:32:57.420
So it’s done like this.

00:32:57.420 --> 00:33:01.520
Tim Ferriss: You’re standing feet shoulder
width apart, roughly.

00:33:01.520 --> 00:33:03.630
Hands flat on the floor with your fingers
interlaced.

00:33:03.630 --> 00:33:06.899
Jason Nemer: Yes, and I would never think
to do a handstand like this.

00:33:06.899 --> 00:33:11.730
I was doing my practice this morning and I’m
like, huh, I wonder how hard that would be?

00:33:11.730 --> 00:33:14.520
It was really fucking hard.

00:33:14.520 --> 00:33:18.330
So 30 years of acrobatic training, I’ve
never seen anybody do that handstand.

00:33:18.330 --> 00:33:21.919
I’m sure it’s been done and I tagged a
few people on Instagram this morning.

00:33:21.919 --> 00:33:25.529
Andrei is one of them; Miguel_Hand_Balance
was another.

00:33:25.529 --> 00:33:28.080
And my third attempt, I figured a couple of
things out.

00:33:28.080 --> 00:33:32.059
Basically, if you let the pinky go wider and
you let the thumb go wider, you have a big

00:33:32.059 --> 00:33:35.120
enough base and you’ve got to start using
the pinky and the thumb.

00:33:35.120 --> 00:33:41.179
But it was just as much as I feel like acrobatics
is very fixed, gymnastics is very fixed, as

00:33:41.179 --> 00:33:47.149
long as the mind is not fixed and if you have
the playfulness and you have a ritual of discovery,

00:33:47.149 --> 00:33:48.760
this practice keeps expanding.

00:33:48.760 --> 00:33:57.120
Tim Ferriss: I love the cross-pollination
when you expose yourself to different physical

00:33:57.120 --> 00:33:58.120
disciplines.

00:33:58.120 --> 00:34:06.039
So you have yoga, we have acrobatics, and
then you have what’s called sports acrobatics,

00:34:06.039 --> 00:34:10.010
circus, Olympic gymnastics for simplicity’s
sake.

00:34:10.010 --> 00:34:12.230
Jason Nemer: Artistic for the people who really
know.

00:34:12.230 --> 00:34:13.230
Tim Ferriss: Sure.

00:34:13.230 --> 00:34:17.609
So very interrelated but different disciplines;
break-dancing.

00:34:17.609 --> 00:34:23.659
So for instance, that position you had right
here reminds me of, I believe it’s called

00:34:23.659 --> 00:34:25.060
the 2000 in break dancing.

00:34:25.060 --> 00:34:26.060
Jason Nemer: Check you out.

00:34:26.060 --> 00:34:31.810
Tim Ferriss: So while you have a 1990, which
is a one-armed spinning handstand, which is

00:34:31.810 --> 00:34:34.139
generally on this portion of the palm.

00:34:34.139 --> 00:34:39.379
It is, mechanically speaking, very similar
to a pirouette even the way they go into it

00:34:39.379 --> 00:34:44.000
with their legs and then bring their legs
together.

00:34:44.000 --> 00:34:49.540
But a 2000, if you found somebody who is very
good at teaching or performing a 2000 and

00:34:49.540 --> 00:34:51.770
explaining it, they would probably have a
lot to add to that conversation.

00:34:51.770 --> 00:34:52.770
Jason Nemer: There you go.

00:34:52.770 --> 00:35:00.770
Tim Ferriss: So if any B boys out there, B
girls, want to add some commentary on how

00:35:00.770 --> 00:35:06.730
to do 2000s properly, you will assist me in,
at some point, having too much to drink and

00:35:06.730 --> 00:35:07.860
injuring myself.

00:35:07.860 --> 00:35:09.820
So that is that.

00:35:09.820 --> 00:35:12.690
I have to just ask, what on earth is shadow
yoga?

00:35:12.690 --> 00:35:13.690
Can you describe this, please?

00:35:13.690 --> 00:35:14.690
Jason Nemer: Sure.

00:35:14.690 --> 00:35:18.890
I love describing this because it’s something
in my recent past, I’d say five years; it’s

00:35:18.890 --> 00:35:22.980
the thing that’s affected my life the most
positively.

00:35:22.980 --> 00:35:26.020
As a gymnast and acrobat, I would have lower
back pain.

00:35:26.020 --> 00:35:29.960
It would come and go, but it was a constant
teacher.

00:35:29.960 --> 00:35:34.960
As soon as I found the shadow practice, for
over five years I wouldn’t say I’ve been

00:35:34.960 --> 00:35:40.180
back pain-free, but if I wake up and I have
back pain, I know if I do my practice and

00:35:40.180 --> 00:35:44.420
I do a number of things that are conducive
to my health, I can clear it.

00:35:44.420 --> 00:35:49.690
So shadow yoga is Zhander Remete is a martial
artist.

00:35:49.690 --> 00:35:50.690
He was in the Army.

00:35:50.690 --> 00:35:51.690
I’m not sure which Army.

00:35:51.690 --> 00:35:53.800
He’s a certified badass.

00:35:53.800 --> 00:35:55.790
He’s such a deep yoga practitioner.

00:35:55.790 --> 00:35:59.500
I’ve actually never met him but Scott, my
teacher, has told me a lot of things.

00:35:59.500 --> 00:36:05.413
One of the things he did in a demo is he took
a deep breath, and then he sat.

00:36:05.413 --> 00:36:08.141
Then he took another deep breath and he sat.

00:36:08.141 --> 00:36:11.240
Then he looks with a big smile and is like,
“Do you know what I did?

00:36:11.240 --> 00:36:12.240
I ate the breath.”

00:36:12.240 --> 00:36:13.560
And he starts laughing.

00:36:13.560 --> 00:36:16.150
Basically, he can just metabolize a breath.

00:36:16.150 --> 00:36:17.520
He doesn’t need to exhale.

00:36:17.520 --> 00:36:20.820
He’s a yoga master, full blown.

00:36:20.820 --> 00:36:23.510
What he did is he studied in Pune from Iyengar.

00:36:23.510 --> 00:36:25.839
Tim Ferriss: What is Pune, is that a place?

00:36:25.839 --> 00:36:26.839
Jason Nemer: Pune is a place.

00:36:26.839 --> 00:36:30.940
Pune is a place in India where Iyengar, who
is a man, who basically, other than Krishnamacharya,

00:36:30.940 --> 00:36:36.530
the father of modern yoga, I would say Iyengar
is one of the most influential people who

00:36:36.530 --> 00:36:39.220
has come to the practice of yoga.

00:36:39.220 --> 00:36:43.590
So he studied with Iyengar for a number of
years, and he also went to the South to study

00:36:43.590 --> 00:36:45.520
Ayurvedic medicine.

00:36:45.520 --> 00:36:50.840
And in Kerala they have temple dancers, bar
nok diem temple dancing and they also have

00:36:50.840 --> 00:36:52.720
Kalari martial artists.

00:36:52.720 --> 00:36:56.210
And the martial artists and the temple dancers
were moving much more in circles.

00:36:56.210 --> 00:37:01.230
He was watching them and he was learning Ayurvedic
science and be basically started to fuse all

00:37:01.230 --> 00:37:02.230
of these things.

00:37:02.230 --> 00:37:07.760
So if I were to give a couple sentences, an
elevator speech of what shadow yoga is, it’s

00:37:07.760 --> 00:37:13.080
based in Iyengar technique for the asanas
and it’s got a Tai Chi feel and there’s

00:37:13.080 --> 00:37:15.170
definitely a lot of circular movements.

00:37:15.170 --> 00:37:20.660
So you start with warming up all the joints
of the body and he said – paraphrased – that

00:37:20.660 --> 00:37:25.560
the martial artists and the temple dancers
for one year just did joint mobilization.

00:37:25.560 --> 00:37:30.420
If you understand how to move all of your
joints very intelligently, then the more complicated

00:37:30.420 --> 00:37:33.440
moves ride on the back of that intelligence.

00:37:33.440 --> 00:37:37.860
So one of the things that makes shadow yoga
unique, as far as I’ve experienced yoga,

00:37:37.860 --> 00:37:42.160
they say if you squeeze the muscle, you block
the prana.

00:37:42.160 --> 00:37:45.210
So if you’re engaging your muscle, you’re
not letting the energy flow.

00:37:45.210 --> 00:37:49.350
So what you do is you try to have your muscles
as soft as possible and you really put your

00:37:49.350 --> 00:37:50.790
attention into the bone layer.

00:37:50.790 --> 00:37:55.420
And you stack your body in ways that the bones
are taken away, the muscles are soft, and

00:37:55.420 --> 00:37:57.760
you start circulating the energy through the
body.

00:37:57.760 --> 00:38:02.910
Tim Ferriss: The bone stacking is a pretty
good segue into all sorts of topics.

00:38:02.910 --> 00:38:07.800
For people who want to learn more about shadow
yoga, is there a particular resources other

00:38:07.800 --> 00:38:09.540
than Google that you would suggest?

00:38:09.540 --> 00:38:11.880
Jason Nemer: Yeah, shadowyoga.com, I believe.

00:38:11.880 --> 00:38:12.880
They have a DVD.

00:38:12.880 --> 00:38:19.220
The unfortunate reality is I think there are
like 20 certified teachers in the world so

00:38:19.220 --> 00:38:21.500
he’s very, very strict on certification.

00:38:21.500 --> 00:38:25.859
I mean talk about Mr. Miyagi, this guy is
the definition of yoga Mr. Miyagi.

00:38:25.859 --> 00:38:28.760
Tim Ferriss: Salty old dog.

00:38:28.760 --> 00:38:31.020
I like those.

00:38:31.020 --> 00:38:37.069
I aspire to be a salty old dog myself so I
like hanging out with the cantankerous, opinionated

00:38:37.069 --> 00:38:39.290
but highly competent salty old dogs.

00:38:39.290 --> 00:38:40.290
Jason Nemer: He’s the guy for you.

00:38:40.290 --> 00:38:42.150
Tim Ferriss: Although yeah, he might not put
up with me.

00:38:42.150 --> 00:38:44.609
That’s a separate issue altogether.

00:38:44.609 --> 00:38:45.609
Bone stacking.

00:38:45.609 --> 00:38:51.359
Let’s talk about handstands for a second
again, and I always mess this up, is it ATB?

00:38:51.359 --> 00:38:52.359
Jason Nemer: ATB.

00:38:52.359 --> 00:38:53.359
Tim Ferriss: Look at me!

00:38:53.359 --> 00:38:54.950
Jason Nemer: You also learned it in Spanish.

00:38:54.950 --> 00:38:55.950
That was AIB.

00:38:55.950 --> 00:38:58.650
Tim Ferriss: AIB, that’s right.

00:38:58.650 --> 00:39:00.319
That was a whole thing in and of itself.

00:39:00.319 --> 00:39:03.320
I did my entire immersion in South America
in Spanish.

00:39:03.320 --> 00:39:06.359
There were a number of people translating
for a handful of folks who only spoke English

00:39:06.359 --> 00:39:11.760
but I found it more entertaining to see how
badly I could mangle things by running on

00:39:11.760 --> 00:39:12.760
the Spanish alone.

00:39:12.760 --> 00:39:14.480
So let’s talking about ATB.

00:39:14.480 --> 00:39:16.790
Should we do a little demo of what that means?

00:39:16.790 --> 00:39:17.790
Jason Nemer: Sure.

00:39:17.790 --> 00:39:19.839
Maybe I’ll talk a little bit, first; I’ll
paint an analogy.

00:39:19.839 --> 00:39:21.480
I like the analogy of bridges.

00:39:21.480 --> 00:39:26.050
Tim Ferriss: So ATB stands for what?

00:39:26.050 --> 00:39:29.630
Jason Nemer: Alignment, tightness and balance.

00:39:29.630 --> 00:39:34.260
I like it in that order because until the
body gets aligned in the most efficient way

00:39:34.260 --> 00:39:38.910
possible, you are losing energy for every
second you’re attempting the handstand.

00:39:38.910 --> 00:39:42.890
So alignment is the Holy Grail of all acrobatics.

00:39:42.890 --> 00:39:47.349
What’s great about these ideas, the ideas
are really simple; the practice is a pain

00:39:47.349 --> 00:39:48.349
in the ass.

00:39:48.349 --> 00:39:51.620
It takes sometimes decades to get a straight
handstand.

00:39:51.620 --> 00:39:55.150
But alignment is straight, straight is strong.

00:39:55.150 --> 00:39:57.140
Our bodies are not made straight.

00:39:57.140 --> 00:40:01.460
Our cells are circular, our bones are not
two by fours.

00:40:01.460 --> 00:40:07.730
So it’s an impossible task that we try to
do to turn our bodies into these knives that

00:40:07.730 --> 00:40:08.730
cut through gravity.

00:40:08.730 --> 00:40:14.640
Tim Ferriss: What are some of the things that
Lu Yi says, his short expressions while people

00:40:14.640 --> 00:40:15.640
are doing handstands?

00:40:15.640 --> 00:40:19.360
Jason Nemer: Today one of his legs is not
working very well; he can’t walk up the

00:40:19.360 --> 00:40:20.400
stairs very well.

00:40:20.400 --> 00:40:21.400
And I joked at him.

00:40:21.400 --> 00:40:23.660
I said, “Lu Yi, your body is not one piece.”

00:40:23.660 --> 00:40:26.050
Because he says, “Make body one piece.”

00:40:26.050 --> 00:40:27.520
Tim Ferriss: One piece, one piece.

00:40:27.520 --> 00:40:29.270
Jason Nemer: Yeah, he likes the body to be
unified.

00:40:29.270 --> 00:40:31.780
And then I have a Russian coach who says,
“Make body one bone.”

00:40:31.780 --> 00:40:32.900
Same, same.

00:40:32.900 --> 00:40:36.670
It’s basically unifying the body and that’s
the second part of the ATB; it’s tightness.

00:40:36.670 --> 00:40:37.670
It’s integration.

00:40:37.670 --> 00:40:42.349
Tim Ferriss: And if you see, for instance
– I know you’ve seen this, but for people

00:40:42.349 --> 00:40:49.240
listening or watching, if you see a photograph
of a gymnast mid-air, or a diver for that

00:40:49.240 --> 00:40:52.000
matter, they’re not relaxed.

00:40:52.000 --> 00:40:54.370
Look at how tight they are.

00:40:54.370 --> 00:40:59.930
Jason Nemer: And a cool experiment for those
of you at home who are interested, is you

00:40:59.930 --> 00:41:02.069
can hard boil an egg and take a raw egg.

00:41:02.069 --> 00:41:04.920
Try to spin them both.

00:41:04.920 --> 00:41:05.990
I don’t want to spoil it.

00:41:05.990 --> 00:41:07.820
Tim Ferriss: You can try that.

00:41:07.820 --> 00:41:10.920
That’s also if you’re an idiot like me
and you hard boil some and you forget which

00:41:10.920 --> 00:41:16.040
ones are raw, spin it and you’ll see very
quickly which ones are hard boiled because

00:41:16.040 --> 00:41:18.200
they’ll fly off the counter, probably.

00:41:18.200 --> 00:41:21.770
Jason Nemer: Another good Lu Yi-ism, and this
is good for anyone to put in their back pocket

00:41:21.770 --> 00:41:27.080
and I’m so happy that Coach Summers also
agrees with me on this: MO extension.

00:41:27.080 --> 00:41:28.609
M-O extension.

00:41:28.609 --> 00:41:30.130
MO extension.

00:41:30.130 --> 00:41:35.621
And what he’s talking about there, it comes
back to Newtonian physics: action and reaction,

00:41:35.621 --> 00:41:37.490
the second law of motion.

00:41:37.490 --> 00:41:39.670
If you push into something, it pushes back.

00:41:39.670 --> 00:41:45.310
So as you push down into the floor, there’s
basically a rebound and this rebound is what

00:41:45.310 --> 00:41:49.540
makes your handstand light and it’s what
makes the body a lot more unified.

00:41:49.540 --> 00:41:50.640
Tim Ferriss: Exactly.

00:41:50.640 --> 00:41:55.839
Also, if we’re going to look at the hierarchy,
if you were to do one – and I’m not saying

00:41:55.839 --> 00:42:03.950
you have to choose one – but if someone
could not somehow wrap their head around the

00:42:03.950 --> 00:42:08.160
alignment piece, if they didn’t have somebody
to help them but I just said you know what,

00:42:08.160 --> 00:42:09.369
forget that.

00:42:09.369 --> 00:42:10.369
More extension.

00:42:10.369 --> 00:42:15.890
No, I’m just going to say it helped me with
my alignment to focus on the extension because

00:42:15.890 --> 00:42:24.990
it took effectively one piece out of the puzzle
because it locked it in place.

00:42:24.990 --> 00:42:30.240
But of course having someone to help with
the alignment is a huge resource.

00:42:30.240 --> 00:42:32.660
But you were talking about bridges.

00:42:32.660 --> 00:42:34.160
Jason Nemer: Yes.

00:42:34.160 --> 00:42:38.410
So a bridge basically can be the analogy of
the body.

00:42:38.410 --> 00:42:42.140
The metal is the bone structure.

00:42:42.140 --> 00:42:44.619
So the first thing they do when they’re
building a bridge is they’ve got to get

00:42:44.619 --> 00:42:48.540
the pylons in place and they have to get the
metal in the right alignment.

00:42:48.540 --> 00:42:53.480
From there, they pour the cement, and the
cement is basically your muscles and the muscles,

00:42:53.480 --> 00:42:57.450
just like the cement, can harden onto those
pieces of metal.

00:42:57.450 --> 00:43:01.150
So basically you align the metal, you pour
the concrete.

00:43:01.150 --> 00:43:03.470
As the concrete hardens, it gets super stable.

00:43:03.470 --> 00:43:06.930
I haven’t really figured out the part of
the analogy of the balance.

00:43:06.930 --> 00:43:08.740
Maybe it has something to do with the tension
of the cables.

00:43:08.740 --> 00:43:10.690
Tim Ferriss: The cables or the fascia.

00:43:10.690 --> 00:43:11.750
Jason Nemer: Yeah.

00:43:11.750 --> 00:43:14.890
But at least the first two have a picture
for you to think about.

00:43:14.890 --> 00:43:21.001
There was a point in gymnastics history in
the ‘70s where the Japanese gymnasts were

00:43:21.001 --> 00:43:26.730
dominant, and they had a lot of techniques
that none of the other world had seen yet.

00:43:26.730 --> 00:43:32.260
One of my friends was very connected to that
lineage and he said that at the Olympics,

00:43:32.260 --> 00:43:35.609
they had a big interview with this guy, like
you have to tell us your secrets.

00:43:35.609 --> 00:43:36.859
How are your gymnasts doing this?

00:43:36.859 --> 00:43:38.520
They’re just blowing us all away.

00:43:38.520 --> 00:43:43.530
He says it’s two things: strength and flexibility.

00:43:43.530 --> 00:43:45.680
And that was pretty much the end of the conversation.

00:43:45.680 --> 00:43:49.950
There was actually a third thing, in my experience;
it’s technique.

00:43:49.950 --> 00:43:54.829
And one of my definitions of technique is
how skillfully you use your strength and your

00:43:54.829 --> 00:43:55.829
flexibility.

00:43:55.829 --> 00:43:59.970
So if somebody doesn’t have a lot of shoulder
mobility, if they’re doing handstands, they

00:43:59.970 --> 00:44:02.130
want to get as much mobility as they can.

00:44:02.130 --> 00:44:06.319
So Coach Summer calls it compression strength?

00:44:06.319 --> 00:44:12.520
Tim Ferriss: In this particular case, he would
want – and it’s always dangerous speaking

00:44:12.520 --> 00:44:18.680
for Coach Summer, one of my favorite salty
old dogs so he can slap me on the internet

00:44:18.680 --> 00:44:19.710
if I get this wrong.

00:44:19.710 --> 00:44:26.700
But if I had to guess, I would say that he
would argue that if you’re going to practice

00:44:26.700 --> 00:44:32.170
handstands correctly, you would want to get
to the point where this type of shoulder flexion

00:44:32.170 --> 00:44:35.170
is comfortable so that you’re not using
a lot of muscle to get here.

00:44:35.170 --> 00:44:36.380
Jason Nemer: Yes, I would agree.

00:44:36.380 --> 00:44:39.940
And whatever flexibility you have, you want
to access it.

00:44:39.940 --> 00:44:42.829
So you want to eventually have a surplus of
shoulder flexibility.

00:44:42.829 --> 00:44:44.589
I was born with circus shoulders.

00:44:44.589 --> 00:44:47.960
So if you have a surplus of the shoulder flexibility,
being vertical is very easy.

00:44:47.960 --> 00:44:49.400
That’s what you want.

00:44:49.400 --> 00:44:54.920
But if you’re stiff, you don’t want to
not get that one-inch of flexibility utilized.

00:44:54.920 --> 00:45:00.790
So basically the technique is using your strength
and flexibility as skillfully as you can with

00:45:00.790 --> 00:45:01.790
what you have.

00:45:01.790 --> 00:45:06.250
In one day, you can’t get stronger or more
flexible, but in one day you can affect your

00:45:06.250 --> 00:45:10.819
approach and affect how skillful you’re
cutting through gravity with the line of your

00:45:10.819 --> 00:45:16.690
body by finding these little hinges and taking
as much of those hinges out.

00:45:16.690 --> 00:45:19.579
Tim Ferriss: What do you think about doing
a little demo?

00:45:19.579 --> 00:45:20.579
Jason Nemer: Yeah.

00:45:20.579 --> 00:45:21.579
Tim Ferriss: Alright.

00:45:21.579 --> 00:45:22.579
Let’s look at ATB.

00:45:22.579 --> 00:45:26.510
I’m thinking what might make sense, is I’ll
do I first.

00:45:26.510 --> 00:45:31.390
Suffice it to say, I don’t think I have
great handstands but they’re trending in

00:45:31.390 --> 00:45:32.390
the right direction.

00:45:32.390 --> 00:45:33.390
Jason Nemer: I agree.

00:45:33.390 --> 00:45:35.260
Tim Ferriss: They’re getting better, which
is all I care about.

00:45:35.260 --> 00:45:39.300
I’m no Bondarenko, at this point.

00:45:39.300 --> 00:45:43.520
Also weigh 100 pounds more; that’s not true.

00:45:43.520 --> 00:45:44.520
I digress.

00:45:44.520 --> 00:45:45.520
Alright.

00:45:45.520 --> 00:45:46.660
Jason Nemer: So foundation.

00:45:46.660 --> 00:45:49.410
You want to have your hands shoulder width
so you can put the hands down and I’ll just

00:45:49.410 --> 00:45:51.190
inspect your foundation.

00:45:51.190 --> 00:45:53.830
This is definitely where Lu Yi will spend
a lot of time.

00:45:53.830 --> 00:45:55.580
Ideally, you’re tenting.

00:45:55.580 --> 00:45:56.950
So this knuckle comes up.

00:45:56.950 --> 00:46:00.010
And what that does is that puts a vector to
root the naughty knuckle.

00:46:00.010 --> 00:46:02.420
Tim Ferriss: This is also very Spiderman-like.

00:46:02.420 --> 00:46:07.630
If you ever see a close-up of Spiderman like
this on a cover, he’s tenting his fingers.

00:46:07.630 --> 00:46:09.530
It’s hard to do without the ground.

00:46:09.530 --> 00:46:13.950
But in other words, if your fingers are completely
flat on the ground, you’re popping up the

00:46:13.950 --> 00:46:19.510
second knuckle while keeping the knuckle of
the palm on the ground.

00:46:19.510 --> 00:46:20.790
Because if this pops up, what’s it called?

00:46:20.790 --> 00:46:21.790
Jason Nemer: The naughty knuckle.

00:46:21.790 --> 00:46:25.079
Tim Ferriss: The naughty knuckle, which is
this index that constantly pops up.

00:46:25.079 --> 00:46:27.660
Jason Nemer: Your hands look great.

00:46:27.660 --> 00:46:30.170
They’re the right distance apart.

00:46:30.170 --> 00:46:31.550
You can go up however you’d like.

00:46:31.550 --> 00:46:33.760
I’ll support you on the way up.

00:46:33.760 --> 00:46:37.410
I’ll let you have your head neutral.

00:46:37.410 --> 00:46:38.410
His head is down.

00:46:38.410 --> 00:46:41.690
I step in between the hands, hug his thighs
and bring the ribs in.

00:46:41.690 --> 00:46:44.930
Domesticate the wall.

00:46:44.930 --> 00:46:45.930
Up, up, up.

00:46:45.930 --> 00:46:46.930
Good.

00:46:46.930 --> 00:46:47.930
Are you breathing?

00:46:47.930 --> 00:46:48.930
Tim Ferriss: Mm-hmm, barely.

00:46:48.930 --> 00:46:49.930
Jason Nemer: Elbows in more.

00:46:49.930 --> 00:46:50.930
Good.

00:46:50.930 --> 00:46:51.930
Looking down with your eyes, fall towards
your fingers a little bit.

00:46:51.930 --> 00:46:52.930
Fall towards, right there, right there.

00:46:52.930 --> 00:46:53.930
Yep, bingo.

00:46:53.930 --> 00:46:55.569
Keep the hands strong, keep the breath.

00:46:55.569 --> 00:46:56.800
Keep falling towards me.

00:46:56.800 --> 00:46:58.660
Yes, right there.

00:46:58.660 --> 00:46:59.900
Right there.

00:46:59.900 --> 00:47:01.119
How you feeling?

00:47:01.119 --> 00:47:02.740
Tim Ferriss: Good.

00:47:02.740 --> 00:47:05.450
I think I’ll come down.

00:47:05.450 --> 00:47:13.000
What I’d love to see, also, is if you could
go up in a handstand, and I’m not going

00:47:13.000 --> 00:47:16.380
to do a great job of spotting; I’m working
on that, also.

00:47:16.380 --> 00:47:20.250
But I’d love for you to show some examples
of common mistakes.

00:47:20.250 --> 00:47:22.580
Jason Nemer: Yep, I love that.

00:47:22.580 --> 00:47:27.190
I’m not going to do the most common mistake,
is taking my hands wider than shoulder width.

00:47:27.190 --> 00:47:30.070
I want to have a vertical line so I’m not
going to compromise that.

00:47:30.070 --> 00:47:32.650
Tim Ferriss: Do you personally point your
middle fingers forward?

00:47:32.650 --> 00:47:33.650
Jason Nemer: I do.

00:47:33.650 --> 00:47:37.720
Tim Ferriss: Some people – quite a few gymnasts
– do index but you prefer middle finger?

00:47:37.720 --> 00:47:41.290
Jason Nemer: Well, and I don’t believe in
right and wrong until you define where you

00:47:41.290 --> 00:47:42.320
want to go.

00:47:42.320 --> 00:47:45.130
And what I teach is partner acrobatic handstands.

00:47:45.130 --> 00:47:49.460
So if you’re practicing with the hands turned
out as a base, I’ll have to have my hands

00:47:49.460 --> 00:47:51.710
turned in; super awkward.

00:47:51.710 --> 00:47:53.150
So the techniques I’m teaching –

00:47:53.150 --> 00:47:54.150
Tim Ferriss: Right, because we’re going
to be –

00:47:54.150 --> 00:47:56.410
Jason Nemer: We’re going to be here.

00:47:56.410 --> 00:47:57.839
Tim Ferriss: This is hand-to-hand grip?

00:47:57.839 --> 00:47:58.940
Jason Nemer: It is.

00:47:58.940 --> 00:48:00.130
Tim Ferriss: Reverse hand to hand.

00:48:00.130 --> 00:48:02.030
Jason Nemer: You can practice with yourself.

00:48:02.030 --> 00:48:03.240
You can’t practice hand to hand by yourself.

00:48:03.240 --> 00:48:04.470
Tim Ferriss: Hand to hand is pretty tough.

00:48:04.470 --> 00:48:07.740
Jason Nemer: Because you need a right hand
with a right hand, and not many people have

00:48:07.740 --> 00:48:08.740
that.

00:48:08.740 --> 00:48:09.740
Tim Ferriss: Right.

00:48:09.740 --> 00:48:13.000
Train as you want to; compete, so to speak.

00:48:13.000 --> 00:48:14.000
Jason Nemer: Exactly.

00:48:14.000 --> 00:48:15.710
Tim Ferriss: Instead of placing them too wide.

00:48:15.710 --> 00:48:18.010
Jason Nemer: Okay, you ready?

00:48:18.010 --> 00:48:22.390
Tim Ferriss: Yes.

00:48:22.390 --> 00:48:27.960
So for those people wondering, that was a
straddle jump, basically.

00:48:27.960 --> 00:48:33.400
Because from here, now if I were to just practice
my coaching here, so we have –

00:48:33.400 --> 00:48:35.270
Jason Nemer: Before anything, I’d bring
the head down.

00:48:35.270 --> 00:48:36.569
Tim Ferriss: Head down, okay.

00:48:36.569 --> 00:48:40.050
Jason Nemer: Because then I have more access
to a straight line in my spine.

00:48:40.050 --> 00:48:41.940
You can turn one of your feet to the side.

00:48:41.940 --> 00:48:42.940
Yep.

00:48:42.940 --> 00:48:44.950
Hug around my thighs with one of your arms.

00:48:44.950 --> 00:48:45.950
Yep.

00:48:45.950 --> 00:48:47.190
Find my ribs with the other hand.

00:48:47.190 --> 00:48:49.180
Now pull everything up and in.

00:48:49.180 --> 00:48:50.180
Bingo.

00:48:50.180 --> 00:48:51.180
That’s it.

00:48:51.180 --> 00:48:52.180
Oops.

00:48:52.180 --> 00:48:53.180
Technical difficulties.

00:48:53.180 --> 00:48:54.240
Tim Ferriss: We’re good.

00:48:54.240 --> 00:48:57.599
And then tightness would be I can pull your
feet apart.

00:48:57.599 --> 00:48:59.290
Jason Nemer: You can also push me down.

00:48:59.290 --> 00:49:00.290
Strong.

00:49:00.290 --> 00:49:01.290
From the thighs, yeah, push me down.

00:49:01.290 --> 00:49:02.290
Tim Ferriss: Push you down?

00:49:02.290 --> 00:49:03.490
Jason Nemer: No, not that way.

00:49:03.490 --> 00:49:04.490
Straight towards –

00:49:04.490 --> 00:49:05.490
Tim Ferriss: Oh, down into the ground.

00:49:05.490 --> 00:49:06.490
Got it.

00:49:06.490 --> 00:49:07.490
Push you down.

00:49:07.490 --> 00:49:08.559
Jason Nemer: Stronger.

00:49:08.559 --> 00:49:09.559
Way stronger.

00:49:09.559 --> 00:49:12.280
Tim Ferriss: Now I’m just going to do some
rotation stuff?

00:49:12.280 --> 00:49:13.280
Jason Nemer: Yes.

00:49:13.280 --> 00:49:15.910
Tim Ferriss: Also doing this kind of stuff.

00:49:15.910 --> 00:49:17.460
And then, let’s see.

00:49:17.460 --> 00:49:20.160
Tightness, balance, and then we can do just
kind of a hot potato.

00:49:20.160 --> 00:49:21.710
Jason Nemer: Yes.

00:49:21.710 --> 00:49:26.099
Tim Ferriss: Alright, so now you can either
come down or we can still demo.

00:49:26.099 --> 00:49:27.990
What are some of the most common mistakes?

00:49:27.990 --> 00:49:32.300
Jason Nemer: I’d say elbows are one of the
most –

00:49:32.300 --> 00:49:33.300
Tim Ferriss: Bent?

00:49:33.300 --> 00:49:34.300
Pointing the wrong way?

00:49:34.300 --> 00:49:35.300
Jason Nemer: Both.

00:49:35.300 --> 00:49:42.190
So saying a straight arm is a very non-descriptive
way to talk about how to position these bones.

00:49:42.190 --> 00:49:48.020
So when you’re down here, basically you
can spin the eyes of your elbow forward towards

00:49:48.020 --> 00:49:50.000
each other, or back.

00:49:50.000 --> 00:49:54.140
What you want to find in a handstand is the
eyes of the elbows are squeezing towards each

00:49:54.140 --> 00:49:55.190
other.

00:49:55.190 --> 00:49:57.140
This gives you a lot of integrity in your
handstands.

00:49:57.140 --> 00:50:00.160
So I’d say elbows from there.

00:50:00.160 --> 00:50:02.470
Probably the head being really far away.

00:50:02.470 --> 00:50:08.030
Tim Ferriss: Because that also just leads
to the cascade of piking at the shoulders

00:50:08.030 --> 00:50:11.520
and then bending too much at the lumbar, trying
to use the legs to correct and then you’re

00:50:11.520 --> 00:50:12.520
done.

00:50:12.520 --> 00:50:16.010
Jason Nemer: So bring the arms up is another
good one, beginner acrobatics.

00:50:16.010 --> 00:50:17.160
Relax.

00:50:17.160 --> 00:50:19.479
Intermediate, advanced.

00:50:19.479 --> 00:50:20.520
Expert is when the shoulders touch the ears.

00:50:20.520 --> 00:50:22.780
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I can’t get that high.

00:50:22.780 --> 00:50:25.400
I’m getting better but not quite.

00:50:25.400 --> 00:50:29.530
Jason Nemer: So if people get the shoulders
and the ears even close to each other, the

00:50:29.530 --> 00:50:33.260
arms in a straight line, that’s going to
take care of a lot of the problems.

00:50:33.260 --> 00:50:37.119
You really shouldn’t coach above the shoulders
for the first several months.

00:50:37.119 --> 00:50:39.240
Tim Ferriss: Shouldn’t coach above the shoulders.

00:50:39.240 --> 00:50:41.350
Jason Nemer: There are a lot of misalignments
from –

00:50:41.350 --> 00:50:44.849
Tim Ferriss: You mean just coaching from basically
here to the fingertips?

00:50:44.849 --> 00:50:46.030
Jason Nemer: Yeah.

00:50:46.030 --> 00:50:49.420
From here down is where most of the big problems
come from.

00:50:49.420 --> 00:50:52.750
So if you’re looking at why they are arching,
it probably has something to do with something

00:50:52.750 --> 00:50:53.900
that’s happening from here down.

00:50:53.900 --> 00:50:57.550
Tim Ferriss: You’ve said to me also there’s
a lot of magic in the hand and the foot placement.

00:50:57.550 --> 00:50:58.550
Jason Nemer: Yes.

00:50:58.550 --> 00:51:02.970
Tim Ferriss: So paying attention to that pays
dividends.

00:51:02.970 --> 00:51:10.240
The next question I wanted to ask you was
– actually, one thing I wanted to just throw

00:51:10.240 --> 00:51:14.940
out there that I found helpful, and this was
at a place called Athletic Playground in the

00:51:14.940 --> 00:51:17.010
East Bay here in Northern California.

00:51:17.010 --> 00:51:18.510
Jason Nemer: Serious place in Emeryville.

00:51:18.510 --> 00:51:19.720
Tim Ferriss: That’s right, in Emeryville.

00:51:19.720 --> 00:51:20.720
Great place.

00:51:20.720 --> 00:51:25.430
I took a handstand class ages ago with a teacher,
Sam.

00:51:25.430 --> 00:51:26.560
Jason Nemer: Sam.

00:51:26.560 --> 00:51:27.560
Amazing teacher.

00:51:27.560 --> 00:51:28.560
Tim Ferriss: Sam was amazing.

00:51:28.560 --> 00:51:32.590
I remember what she had us do at one point,
and it was just such a simple idea.

00:51:32.590 --> 00:51:37.450
And it was this: basically, she had us traversing
the room, going back and forth.

00:51:37.450 --> 00:51:40.280
I’m not going to do it right now but I like
to walk.

00:51:40.280 --> 00:51:44.570
She had us in rows and going back and forth.

00:51:44.570 --> 00:51:47.220
We were supposed to go up into a handstand.

00:51:47.220 --> 00:51:48.500
Now, it could be only 45 degrees.

00:51:48.500 --> 00:51:53.880
If you’re a beginner – lest you topple
over, just go up 45 degrees.

00:51:53.880 --> 00:51:58.150
And if I went up with this leg, I would come
down with this leg.

00:51:58.150 --> 00:52:00.700
So it’s sort of like a scissor at the top.

00:52:00.700 --> 00:52:04.150
And what she noticed is that people would
walk like this, and then they would put their

00:52:04.150 --> 00:52:07.940
hands out, kind of like Frankenstein, and
then go into it.

00:52:07.940 --> 00:52:10.650
She said all right, let’s try something
different, because people were flipping and

00:52:10.650 --> 00:52:11.650
flopping everywhere.

00:52:11.650 --> 00:52:14.839
She said I want you to do the same thing again,
but instead of that you’re going to start

00:52:14.839 --> 00:52:18.040
with your arms way up over your head, shoulders
elevated.

00:52:18.040 --> 00:52:19.970
This doesn’t move at all.

00:52:19.970 --> 00:52:21.940
And I want you to do the exact, same thing.

00:52:21.940 --> 00:52:24.070
And all of a sudden, just stability 10Xed.

00:52:24.070 --> 00:52:25.150
It was incredible.

00:52:25.150 --> 00:52:27.059
Jason Nemer: That’s basic tumbling.

00:52:27.059 --> 00:52:31.460
That’s lunge, lever lunge is how I knew
it as.

00:52:31.460 --> 00:52:36.700
It’s basically you make a straight line,
you bring a leg up and nothing hinges.

00:52:36.700 --> 00:52:42.299
Your body just falls forward, you hit that
line and on the way out, as well.

00:52:42.299 --> 00:52:45.970
So the ability to keep the body, again, in
one piece is what you’re looking for.

00:52:45.970 --> 00:52:46.970
Tim Ferriss: One bone.

00:52:46.970 --> 00:52:48.390
Jason Nemer: One bone.

00:52:48.390 --> 00:52:55.770
Tim Ferriss: What other teachers or mentors
have had a big impact on you?

00:52:55.770 --> 00:52:59.799
Jason Nemer: Dharma Mittra, yogically has
been an amazing teacher.

00:52:59.799 --> 00:53:02.750
He’s a Brazilian yoga teacher.

00:53:02.750 --> 00:53:09.250
He was born and raised in Brazil and he came
to New York and met his guru, Yoga Gupta,

00:53:09.250 --> 00:53:14.430
in I want to say the mid-’70s?

00:53:14.430 --> 00:53:19.430
Out of everyone that I’ve met in the yoga
world, he does the practice.

00:53:19.430 --> 00:53:20.430
He’s super deep.

00:53:20.430 --> 00:53:22.690
He teaches psychic development techniques.

00:53:22.690 --> 00:53:24.940
Tim Ferriss: Psychic development techniques?

00:53:24.940 --> 00:53:26.599
What are we talking about, here?

00:53:26.599 --> 00:53:27.599
Jason Nemer: Yeah.

00:53:27.599 --> 00:53:30.860
There are things that he learned from his
guru.

00:53:30.860 --> 00:53:35.520
The practice is a lot of visualization and
breathing techniques, some humming sounds

00:53:35.520 --> 00:53:38.150
that unlock vibration in the head.

00:53:38.150 --> 00:53:43.570
Tim Ferriss: Which is a trippy thing to do
in a sensory deprivation tank, actually, which

00:53:43.570 --> 00:53:44.740
I’ve experimented with a bit.

00:53:44.740 --> 00:53:46.760
Jason Nemer: I bet, I bet.

00:53:46.760 --> 00:53:52.400
Tim Ferriss: That is a full-blown psychedelic
auditory experience without psychedelics but

00:53:52.400 --> 00:53:54.190
that’s maybe for a different podcast.

00:53:54.190 --> 00:53:57.790
Jason Nemer: You know, vibration is vibration;
there’s a lot of ways to unlock those things.

00:53:57.790 --> 00:53:59.130
Tim Ferriss: Hitachi magic wand?

00:53:59.130 --> 00:54:00.130
Jason Nemer: Exactly.

00:54:00.130 --> 00:54:01.130
You have one, too?

00:54:01.130 --> 00:54:07.869
Tim Ferriss: Which, by the way, was recommend
to me by a Russian medical massage expert,

00:54:07.869 --> 00:54:14.930
and not in the red light district, for relaxing
hypertonic forearms.

00:54:14.930 --> 00:54:19.590
The original version, plug-in version, which
you can get at your friendly neighborhood

00:54:19.590 --> 00:54:24.200
sex store, on high as it turns on.

00:54:24.200 --> 00:54:28.760
I’m going to get my terms wrong, here.

00:54:28.760 --> 00:54:30.550
Was that, not swami, guru, no.

00:54:30.550 --> 00:54:31.550
Something Gupta.

00:54:31.550 --> 00:54:32.550
Jason Nemer: Yoga Gupta.

00:54:32.550 --> 00:54:33.550
Tim Ferriss: Yoga Gupta.

00:54:33.550 --> 00:54:34.550
Are you referring to –

00:54:34.550 --> 00:54:35.740
Jason Nemer: His guru.

00:54:35.740 --> 00:54:36.740
Tim Ferriss: His guru.

00:54:36.740 --> 00:54:37.740
Jason Nemer: Who’s an Indian guy.

00:54:37.740 --> 00:54:38.740
Tim Ferriss: Got it.

00:54:38.740 --> 00:54:44.859
Jason Nemer: It’s basically a lot of really
ancient knowledge that to some degree, a lot

00:54:44.859 --> 00:54:48.790
of the yoga knowledge is able to be accessed
on the web.

00:54:48.790 --> 00:54:50.390
You can really look.

00:54:50.390 --> 00:54:54.750
Acrobatics still is not; it’s still really
hard to find any valuable acrobatic written

00:54:54.750 --> 00:54:56.280
text.

00:54:56.280 --> 00:55:03.920
The thing that’s valuable about yoga masters
is they communicate things energetically.

00:55:03.920 --> 00:55:08.070
This is a saying that comes from Dharma Mittra
that came from his guru: “Once blessed is

00:55:08.070 --> 00:55:12.309
a student that can copy the teacher physically;
twice blessed is a student that can copy them

00:55:12.309 --> 00:55:14.319
physically and mentally.

00:55:14.319 --> 00:55:18.549
Three times blessed is a student that can
copy them physically, mentally and spiritually.”

00:55:18.549 --> 00:55:23.250
There was a day where the guru was gone and
somebody said, when is he coming back?

00:55:23.250 --> 00:55:30.000
And Dharma started to move, as the guru does,
and started to speak with the same tone of

00:55:30.000 --> 00:55:32.990
voice: “The guru will come back on May 31st.”

00:55:32.990 --> 00:55:34.710
Are there 31 days in May?

00:55:34.710 --> 00:55:36.720
Tim Ferriss: Let’s just pretend there are.

00:55:36.720 --> 00:55:38.810
Jason Nemer: And sure enough, he came back.

00:55:38.810 --> 00:55:45.640
So he’s a teacher of these very mystical
aspects of yoga and at the same time, he does

00:55:45.640 --> 00:55:46.981
all kinds of crazy asanas.

00:55:46.981 --> 00:55:48.670
Asanas are poses.

00:55:48.670 --> 00:55:52.400
He has a poster of 908 yoga postures.

00:55:52.400 --> 00:55:56.320
So when I saw this poster, I was like, I have
to meet this guy.

00:55:56.320 --> 00:56:03.140
When I finally met him, the types of teachings
were much more fulfilling and deep than physical

00:56:03.140 --> 00:56:04.140
poses.

00:56:04.140 --> 00:56:06.970
It was really philosophies on life.

00:56:06.970 --> 00:56:10.599
The way that he interacts with the world,
I just watch how he eats a salad.

00:56:10.599 --> 00:56:14.299
He looks at every piece of food before he
puts it in his mouth, the mindfulness practice

00:56:14.299 --> 00:56:16.190
and the compassion.

00:56:16.190 --> 00:56:17.200
So yeah, Dharma Mittra.

00:56:17.200 --> 00:56:18.550
Tim Ferriss: Big influence.

00:56:18.550 --> 00:56:21.109
Jason Nemer: He’s 77, the same age as Lu
Yi.

00:56:21.109 --> 00:56:24.250
The two of them haven’t met but I’ve got
my 77-year-old masters.

00:56:24.250 --> 00:56:26.520
Tim Ferriss: Do you think they would love
each other or kill each other?

00:56:26.520 --> 00:56:28.359
Jason Nemer: No, they would love each other
for sure.

00:56:28.359 --> 00:56:31.760
And I’ve seen Lu Yi with a Thai massage
master.

00:56:31.760 --> 00:56:33.849
I was really interested to see how that would
go.

00:56:33.849 --> 00:56:39.470
It’s kind of like in Star Wars Return of
the Jedi when you’ve got the party at the

00:56:39.470 --> 00:56:44.530
end, and you’ve got Anakin and Obi Wan and
basically all the dead Jedi masters hanging

00:56:44.530 --> 00:56:45.530
out in spirit.

00:56:45.530 --> 00:56:49.980
When I see masters together, I swear that’s
what it seems like because those magical spirits

00:56:49.980 --> 00:56:53.760
are getting together and just celebrating
life together.

00:56:53.760 --> 00:57:04.700
Tim Ferriss: Well, actually, before we get
back to the acrobatics, I want to ask you

00:57:04.700 --> 00:57:10.200
about contemporary yoga, as it were; just
the state of yoga in the United States, popular

00:57:10.200 --> 00:57:12.040
yoga.

00:57:12.040 --> 00:57:16.350
I was put off by yoga for a very, very long
time.

00:57:16.350 --> 00:57:20.590
And maybe this is going to be like speaking
out against the church.

00:57:20.590 --> 00:57:21.590
I don’t know.

00:57:21.590 --> 00:57:23.119
I don’t want to create any problems.

00:57:23.119 --> 00:57:24.730
But I’m curious.

00:57:24.730 --> 00:57:30.990
What are things that you see in the yoga world
that drive you crazy?

00:57:30.990 --> 00:57:35.030
Aside from not pointing their feet when they
do handstands.

00:57:35.030 --> 00:57:38.400
Jason Nemer: No, it’s job security for me.

00:57:38.400 --> 00:57:42.270
The worse they do at handstands, the more
clients I’m going to get so I’m not upset

00:57:42.270 --> 00:57:45.280
at their handstand techniques; that’s actually
great.

00:57:45.280 --> 00:57:51.270
I feel just like religion, yoga can get very
one way; it’s my way or the highway.

00:57:51.270 --> 00:57:53.710
This is the right way and everything else
is wrong.

00:57:53.710 --> 00:57:58.329
That’s challenging to me about some yoga
systems and people.

00:57:58.329 --> 00:58:05.770
I also feel that there’s a ceiling on yoga,
and the ceiling is you have all this amazing

00:58:05.770 --> 00:58:10.230
knowledge, and all this amazing practice but
how are you bringing that into the world?

00:58:10.230 --> 00:58:11.750
What happens when you’re in traffic?

00:58:11.750 --> 00:58:12.839
How are you with your mom?

00:58:12.839 --> 00:58:14.280
Do you talk to your mom?

00:58:14.280 --> 00:58:16.119
Do you tell her the truth?

00:58:16.119 --> 00:58:21.789
So I feel like AcroYoga and acrobatics and
other partner practices help to take that

00:58:21.789 --> 00:58:26.130
wisdom that people spend so long cultivating
and they give it life; they give it access.

00:58:26.130 --> 00:58:27.520
Tim Ferriss: That’s a good point.

00:58:27.520 --> 00:58:30.089
You get to rehearse the human interaction
element.

00:58:30.089 --> 00:58:34.309
It’s like, well that’s great that you’re
listening to a dharma talk and sitting on

00:58:34.309 --> 00:58:38.900
the floor and doing your poses, and listening
to a speech about compassion.

00:58:38.900 --> 00:58:42.770
But when you go to a restaurant and flip out
at your server because they didn’t get you

00:58:42.770 --> 00:58:50.380
water in two minutes instead of their normal
five or whatever it might be, I never really

00:58:50.380 --> 00:58:51.809
thought about that.

00:58:51.809 --> 00:58:57.010
The community aspect and the communal practice
of Acro is one of the things that has kept

00:58:57.010 --> 00:59:05.700
me with it, quite frankly because in many
worlds, there is a high degree of skepticism

00:59:05.700 --> 00:59:09.140
or even aggression towards outsiders or visitors.

00:59:09.140 --> 00:59:14.740
And you see this – not always – but in,
for instance, some – but not all – jiu-jitsu

00:59:14.740 --> 00:59:15.740
schools.

00:59:15.740 --> 00:59:20.500
An exception would be something like Marcelo
Garcia jiu-jitsu in New York where they’re

00:59:20.500 --> 00:59:27.339
very welcoming but they have a zero tolerance
for bullshit or machismo or bullying.

00:59:27.339 --> 00:59:38.349
to me that I have – and maybe this is just
delusional – a built-in group of friends.

00:59:38.349 --> 00:59:48.069
And the fact that what it sounds like you
were alluding to is that the Acro helps you

00:59:48.069 --> 00:59:53.390
to practice things that otherwise might be
compartmentalized to a bunch of poses by yourself

00:59:53.390 --> 00:59:56.000
on a yoga mat.

00:59:56.000 --> 01:00:03.089
I think that group practice or partner practice
also has led – and I don’t have any involvement

01:00:03.089 --> 01:00:09.789
with the non-Acro yoga community but a lot
of the Acro folks, they do almost everything

01:00:09.789 --> 01:00:10.789
together.

01:00:10.789 --> 01:00:12.020
It’s not just the yoga.

01:00:12.020 --> 01:00:18.560
It’s like they have group meals, they cook,
they do fundraisers, they help each other’s

01:00:18.560 --> 01:00:20.119
companies.

01:00:20.119 --> 01:00:24.609
That sort of tribal cohesion is something
that I think – and this was driven home

01:00:24.609 --> 01:00:29.420
for me when I interviewed Sebastian Junger
on the podcast – is something that I think

01:00:29.420 --> 01:00:33.680
we need as social creatures.

01:00:33.680 --> 01:00:35.150
I could go on and on.

01:00:35.150 --> 01:00:37.680
Jason Nemer: I’m so happy you’re going
on and on.

01:00:37.680 --> 01:00:43.380
And for you, when I first met you, I felt
that I really hoped that you actually stepped

01:00:43.380 --> 01:00:48.349
towards the Acro community because I feel
like people that are celebrity status, people

01:00:48.349 --> 01:00:53.539
who are very good at what they do, it’s
really hard for a lot of those people to really

01:00:53.539 --> 01:00:58.160
let their guard down and to feel accepted
as the human they are versus the titles that

01:00:58.160 --> 01:00:59.160
they are.

01:00:59.160 --> 01:01:02.400
So the fact that you’re reflecting this
means that what we’ve done to build this

01:01:02.400 --> 01:01:08.309
community is working because everyone’s
a freak in so many ways.

01:01:08.309 --> 01:01:12.980
What happens when you practice AcroYoga is
you still are practicing yoga; you still are

01:01:12.980 --> 01:01:16.650
doing sola practices and you’re understanding
where your blockages are.

01:01:16.650 --> 01:01:21.060
If you’re a big asshole, the community is
a self-regulating machine.

01:01:21.060 --> 01:01:24.940
If there’s one person that keeps standing
out in the community, they’re going to get

01:01:24.940 --> 01:01:26.440
told from many different people.

01:01:26.440 --> 01:01:31.200
So we really hold a standard of evolution
and the way that we interact with each other

01:01:31.200 --> 01:01:35.990
gives us the potential to keep evolving in
the direction that we want to as a community,

01:01:35.990 --> 01:01:41.330
which is the counterculture to the iPhones,
to the Instagram, to the Facebook.

01:01:41.330 --> 01:01:43.180
And I think those things are all amazing.

01:01:43.180 --> 01:01:47.960
I think it’s really important that we learn
how to use technology in a way that leverages

01:01:47.960 --> 01:01:53.670
more connection.

01:01:53.670 --> 01:01:59.569
And I feel like there’s a big hunger for
real, authentic, wholesome human connection.

01:01:59.569 --> 01:02:01.420
I feel like that’s what we offer.

01:02:01.420 --> 01:02:10.539
Tim Ferriss: What do you think about doing
a little bit of, I guess you would call it

01:02:10.539 --> 01:02:11.539
solar?

01:02:11.539 --> 01:02:12.539
Jason Nemer: Acrobatic flying?

01:02:12.539 --> 01:02:13.539
Tim Ferriss: Acrobatic flying?

01:02:13.539 --> 01:02:14.869
Jason Nemer: Are you basing or flying, or
both?

01:02:14.869 --> 01:02:15.880
Tim Ferriss: Either.

01:02:15.880 --> 01:02:16.880
I’ll fly.

01:02:16.880 --> 01:02:23.170
I’ve only been doing basing for the last
couple of months so this ought to be entertaining.

01:02:23.170 --> 01:02:26.329
I’ll be the spry flyer.

01:02:26.329 --> 01:02:29.730
But I don’t know what you think would be
of interest of folks.

01:02:29.730 --> 01:02:34.450
I’m just going to demo something really
quick, don’t know which.

01:02:34.450 --> 01:02:35.450
Primary?

01:02:35.450 --> 01:02:41.570
So what I’d encourage people to think about,
just so we have some shared vocabulary here,

01:02:41.570 --> 01:02:46.390
is think of bending at the hips as piking.

01:02:46.390 --> 01:02:48.050
This is very over simplified.

01:02:48.050 --> 01:02:50.289
Legs apart, straddle.

01:02:50.289 --> 01:02:54.730
So this is going to be a very common position.

01:02:54.730 --> 01:03:02.430
You can think of it just as like sitting down
on the floor with your legs spread and back

01:03:02.430 --> 01:03:03.599
straight.

01:03:03.599 --> 01:03:05.520
This gives you a 90-degree angle here.

01:03:05.520 --> 01:03:07.320
This is shelving.

01:03:07.320 --> 01:03:10.670
It is going to be used a lot in acrobatics.

01:03:10.670 --> 01:03:14.140
Just so that I don’t have to explain that
when I’m upside down.

01:03:14.140 --> 01:03:17.900
Jason Nemer: With that great description,
I think we should play with corkscrews.

01:03:17.900 --> 01:03:18.900
Tim Ferriss: Corkscrews, okay.

01:03:18.900 --> 01:03:20.230
Jason Nemer: Have you done corkscrews?

01:03:20.230 --> 01:03:23.029
Tim Ferriss: I’ve based it; I don’t know
if I’ve flown it.

01:03:23.029 --> 01:03:24.470
Jason Nemer: Today is your lucky day.

01:03:24.470 --> 01:03:26.560
Tim Ferriss: Today’s my lucky day, okay.

01:03:26.560 --> 01:03:28.040
Jason Nemer: Let’s go star.

01:03:28.040 --> 01:03:29.040
Tim Ferriss: Star?

01:03:29.040 --> 01:03:30.040
Yep.

01:03:30.040 --> 01:03:31.040
So hand-to-hand grip.

01:03:31.040 --> 01:03:33.560
I’m going to have these two fingers on the
wrists.

01:03:33.560 --> 01:03:37.160
Jason Nemer: Basically I want to keep the
arms strong and the legs receptive, and you

01:03:37.160 --> 01:03:39.490
can jump when you’re ready.

01:03:39.490 --> 01:03:41.420
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:03:41.420 --> 01:03:52.920
Jason Nemer: Let’s do up and down to straddle
that a few times, just to calibrate.

01:03:52.920 --> 01:03:56.770
Tim Ferriss: Sure.

01:03:56.770 --> 01:04:04.150
Jason Nemer: I’m going to go to the other
side.

01:04:04.150 --> 01:04:06.190
The other side is this one.

01:04:06.190 --> 01:04:07.190
Alright.

01:04:07.190 --> 01:04:08.190
Let’s go 180 to front plank.

01:04:08.190 --> 01:04:09.190
Good.

01:04:09.190 --> 01:04:10.190
That’s it.

01:04:10.190 --> 01:04:11.190
Arms strong.

01:04:11.190 --> 01:04:12.190
Switch the hands.

01:04:12.190 --> 01:04:13.190
Switch again.

01:04:13.190 --> 01:04:14.190
Straddle and go slow.

01:04:14.190 --> 01:04:16.869
I’ve got you.

01:04:16.869 --> 01:04:18.160
Look at you, twinkle toes.

01:04:18.160 --> 01:04:19.210
Nice one!

01:04:19.210 --> 01:04:20.210
Awesome.

01:04:20.210 --> 01:04:21.319
Tim Ferriss: Thanks.

01:04:21.319 --> 01:04:23.950
Jason Nemer: One more time?

01:04:23.950 --> 01:04:25.530
Tim Ferriss: Sure.

01:04:25.530 --> 01:04:27.109
Jason Nemer: Good.

01:04:27.109 --> 01:04:29.220
Arms strong, belly down.

01:04:29.220 --> 01:04:30.800
Switch the hand.

01:04:30.800 --> 01:04:31.849
Then second.

01:04:31.849 --> 01:04:34.180
Straddle wide, nice and slow.

01:04:34.180 --> 01:04:36.130
Nice.

01:04:36.130 --> 01:04:39.530
We’ve got a bust to hand-to-hand; we’re
here.

01:04:39.530 --> 01:04:40.950
Tim Ferriss: Hand to hand?

01:04:40.950 --> 01:04:41.950
Jason Nemer: Yep.

01:04:41.950 --> 01:04:42.950
Straighten your arms; don’t think about
it.

01:04:42.950 --> 01:04:45.069
Strong arms, even stronger elbows.

01:04:45.069 --> 01:04:47.390
Fall towards my feet, right there.

01:04:47.390 --> 01:04:49.819
Mr. Tim Ferris, doing a hand to hand.

01:04:49.819 --> 01:04:51.289
Elbows in more, arms in more.

01:04:51.289 --> 01:04:52.920
Newton, push down, push down.

01:04:52.920 --> 01:04:53.920
Yes.

01:04:53.920 --> 01:04:54.920
Yeah, buddy.

01:04:54.920 --> 01:04:55.920
Tim Ferriss: Whoa, that was good.

01:04:55.920 --> 01:04:56.920
Jason Nemer: You even saved the day.

01:04:56.920 --> 01:04:57.920
Right on.

01:04:57.920 --> 01:04:58.920
Tim Ferriss: Alright.

01:04:58.920 --> 01:05:00.049
Jason Nemer: You’re a flyer now!

01:05:00.049 --> 01:05:02.069
Tim Ferriss: Thanks, man.

01:05:02.069 --> 01:05:03.089
I’m trying.

01:05:03.089 --> 01:05:05.109
Jason Nemer: It’s official.

01:05:05.109 --> 01:05:07.140
Tim Ferriss: I’m trying.

01:05:07.140 --> 01:05:16.859
I need to stop drinking gallons of cream every
day and take some weight off these thunder

01:05:16.859 --> 01:05:17.859
thighs.

01:05:17.859 --> 01:05:18.859
That was good.

01:05:18.859 --> 01:05:20.299
Jason Nemer: Your fat is my fit.

01:05:20.299 --> 01:05:23.260
Tim Ferriss: Your fat is my fit.

01:05:23.260 --> 01:05:24.260
That’s true.

01:05:24.260 --> 01:05:31.200
I was saying, I was actually working with
a flyer, very gifted flyer.

01:05:31.200 --> 01:05:33.510
I don’t know if you’ve met Kiplinn?

01:05:33.510 --> 01:05:34.510
Jason Nemer: No.

01:05:34.510 --> 01:05:39.150
Tim Ferriss: Kiplinn Sagmiller, @Kiplinn on
all the social, K-I-P-L-I-N-N.

01:05:39.150 --> 01:05:41.260
Very gifted.

01:05:41.260 --> 01:05:48.190
I said to her at one point, “Kiplinn, you
realize that this routine is starting to get

01:05:48.190 --> 01:05:49.710
easy; I need you to eat more.”

01:05:49.710 --> 01:05:56.920
If this is going to be progressive resistance
for me, I need you to gain like ten pounds

01:05:56.920 --> 01:05:58.990
a month for the next three to five months.

01:05:58.990 --> 01:06:00.200
Jason Nemer: Maybe she should just get pregnant.

01:06:00.200 --> 01:06:03.529
Tim Ferriss: That’s an expensive way to
train.

01:06:03.529 --> 01:06:04.779
Jason Nemer: True.

01:06:04.779 --> 01:06:07.510
Tim Ferriss: So a couple of questions for
you.

01:06:07.510 --> 01:06:13.599
I want to talk about challenging times, just
getting out of some of the technical stuff.

01:06:13.599 --> 01:06:15.599
And I apologize for having a very flat hand.

01:06:15.599 --> 01:06:17.049
I was thinking about the hand.

01:06:17.049 --> 01:06:18.260
I haven’t flown in awhile.

01:06:18.260 --> 01:06:23.140
One of the things, actually, we might as well
explain this.

01:06:23.140 --> 01:06:24.530
Do you want to explain it?

01:06:24.530 --> 01:06:25.530
Jason Nemer: Sure.

01:06:25.530 --> 01:06:27.809
Actually, let’s stand up for this one.

01:06:27.809 --> 01:06:30.859
So the way that humans connect is the hardest
part in acrobatics.

01:06:30.859 --> 01:06:34.340
We have more bones in our hands and feet than
the rest of our body so finding all these

01:06:34.340 --> 01:06:39.390
micro angles is like wine; it gets better
with age and practice.

01:06:39.390 --> 01:06:45.290
So if we have a straight arm line, if we go
really flat, what that means is like we’re

01:06:45.290 --> 01:06:48.549
doing it on the ground and we feel a lot of
compression in the wrists.

01:06:48.549 --> 01:06:53.040
If we go really steep, just like a steep angle,
we’ll feel it in the thumb girdle.

01:06:53.040 --> 01:06:57.049
And we find that Goldilocks happy medium.

01:06:57.049 --> 01:07:00.170
Little more flex; flatter, good.

01:07:00.170 --> 01:07:05.260
And we lean into each other and really feel
not only our body as one piece but our collective

01:07:05.260 --> 01:07:06.529
arm line as one piece.

01:07:06.529 --> 01:07:07.760
That’s where the money’s at.

01:07:07.760 --> 01:07:09.410
Tim Ferriss: You can calibrate that.

01:07:09.410 --> 01:07:10.839
Do you want to do a quick calibration?

01:07:10.839 --> 01:07:12.470
Jason Nemer: I’ll tuck sit on you?

01:07:12.470 --> 01:07:13.470
Tim Ferriss: Sure.

01:07:13.470 --> 01:07:14.569
Jason Nemer: You’ve got to base.

01:07:14.569 --> 01:07:16.609
You’ve got to earn your paycheck.

01:07:16.609 --> 01:07:20.260
I going to not drop this.

01:07:20.260 --> 01:07:23.740
Tim Ferriss: The mic right on my front teeth;
that’d be great.

01:07:23.740 --> 01:07:27.339
Jason Nemer: So what’s great about this
is there’s not a lot of risk involved as

01:07:27.339 --> 01:07:30.829
I just bring my feet up a little bit, testing
the water.

01:07:30.829 --> 01:07:34.760
If that goes well, a little more advanced
would be a tuck.

01:07:34.760 --> 01:07:40.520
If that goes well, you can baby hand to hand.

01:07:40.520 --> 01:07:41.520
Arms more forward.

01:07:41.520 --> 01:07:42.520
Forward, forward.

01:07:42.520 --> 01:07:43.920
Yeah, keep coming.

01:07:43.920 --> 01:07:44.940
Right there.

01:07:44.940 --> 01:07:45.940
Better.

01:07:45.940 --> 01:07:46.940
Awesome.

01:07:46.940 --> 01:07:47.940
Your quality grip is nice.

01:07:47.940 --> 01:07:48.940
Tim Ferriss: Nice.

01:07:48.940 --> 01:07:49.940
Thanks, man.

01:07:49.940 --> 01:07:50.940
It’s getting there.

01:07:50.940 --> 01:07:57.920
So the testing here, and picking your feet
off the ground, and you can also do little

01:07:57.920 --> 01:08:02.410
movements just to help activate the stabilizers
in the shoulder.

01:08:02.410 --> 01:08:07.890
Jason Nemer: And what’s cool about the way
that we train with humans is there’s no

01:08:07.890 --> 01:08:11.059
machine in a gym that can teach you how to
unify your body.

01:08:11.059 --> 01:08:16.020
There’s no machine that can teach you how
to have a really strong line because it takes

01:08:16.020 --> 01:08:21.580
human chaos to create the stability to match
that chaos.

01:08:21.580 --> 01:08:28.300
Tim Ferriss: There’s a rig that I’m pretty
sure I came up with, but who knows.

01:08:28.300 --> 01:08:29.300
Jason Nemer: Fairban rig?

01:08:29.300 --> 01:08:31.100
Tim Ferriss: Well, actually, hold on a second.

01:08:31.100 --> 01:08:32.270
Jason Nemer: That could work.

01:08:32.270 --> 01:08:36.350
Tim Ferriss: The way that I put it together,
I was chatting with Coach Sommer, who helped

01:08:36.350 --> 01:08:41.630
me set up this incredible rings contraption
that we were talking a little bit about, which

01:08:41.630 --> 01:08:43.589
includes something called the 50/50.

01:08:43.589 --> 01:08:45.600
You call it the dream machine.

01:08:45.600 --> 01:08:46.600
Jason Nemer: Yes.

01:08:46.600 --> 01:08:48.609
Thank God for the dream machine.

01:08:48.609 --> 01:08:49.949
Tim Ferriss: It’s incredible.

01:08:49.949 --> 01:08:52.040
So imagine you have a harness around your
waist.

01:08:52.040 --> 01:08:54.960
It looks something like a rock climbing harness.

01:08:54.960 --> 01:08:56.710
This one is from Sam, I think it is.

01:08:56.710 --> 01:09:00.739
A lot of Sam in this episode.

01:09:00.739 --> 01:09:09.620
You clip that to a cable that then goes up
over a pulley and then down to the rings.

01:09:09.620 --> 01:09:14.810
That allows you to work with effectively half
of your body weight; let’s just call it.

01:09:14.810 --> 01:09:19.770
Therefore you can progress in movements that
you might not be able to even attempt safely,

01:09:19.770 --> 01:09:26.140
such as an iron cross, or a Maltese or a Victorian,
a Vic or any number of things; back lever,

01:09:26.140 --> 01:09:27.140
etc.

01:09:27.140 --> 01:09:28.179
But let’s just say that’s too intense.

01:09:28.179 --> 01:09:31.429
Because right now, I have medial epicondylitis
in both elbows.

01:09:31.429 --> 01:09:35.570
I also have severely inflamed right elbow
bursa.

01:09:35.570 --> 01:09:38.199
This is olecranon bursa on the right side.

01:09:38.199 --> 01:09:41.469
And putting aside all the weird drugs and
injections and so on that we might explore,

01:09:41.469 --> 01:09:47.299
I’m going to put that as a footnote for
a future episode.

01:09:47.299 --> 01:09:53.150
The device that Coach Sommer helped me to
install involves power levers.

01:09:53.150 --> 01:09:57.400
So power levers – and I’ll put out some
photos and videos of this at this point for

01:09:57.400 --> 01:09:58.520
people to check out – are awesome.

01:09:58.520 --> 01:10:01.570
So now it’s in combination with the 50/50.

01:10:01.570 --> 01:10:09.830
And you’ll see some people mimic this by
using straps, or they’ll fuss with the rings

01:10:09.830 --> 01:10:16.239
in such a way that the rings are effectively
mid-forearm.

01:10:16.239 --> 01:10:19.690
The power lever looks like a robo cop glove.

01:10:19.690 --> 01:10:21.380
Jason Nemer: I already like it.

01:10:21.380 --> 01:10:23.230
Tim Ferriss: It’s made out of metal.

01:10:23.230 --> 01:10:25.870
There’s a strap that goes around the forearm.

01:10:25.870 --> 01:10:31.231
On top of the forearm, you have basically
a uniform ridge of let’s just call it a

01:10:31.231 --> 01:10:33.750
half inch that goes down to the top of the
hand.

01:10:33.750 --> 01:10:39.410
Then perpendicularly, you have these holes
drilled – let’s just call it ten holes

01:10:39.410 --> 01:10:43.160
– that go from the elbow to the fist.

01:10:43.160 --> 01:10:44.760
Now, what does this allow you to do?

01:10:44.760 --> 01:10:50.449
It allows you to take off the rings and then
clip in the ring strap at your elbow.

01:10:50.449 --> 01:10:54.730
So now, the point of support is closer to
the fulcrum of the shoulder.

01:10:54.730 --> 01:10:55.930
Jason Nemer: That’s even easier.

01:10:55.930 --> 01:10:57.380
Tim Ferriss: It’s much, much easier.

01:10:57.380 --> 01:11:02.290
But over time, you can then just move it out,
one notch at a time or two notches at a time.

01:11:02.290 --> 01:11:07.270
That’s a little off topic related to the
handstands, but it’s just been an incredible

01:11:07.270 --> 01:11:08.620
boon for training.

01:11:08.620 --> 01:11:13.350
Especially if let’s just say you’re trying
to attempt a movement like a proper ring dip

01:11:13.350 --> 01:11:18.050
with a support position at the top with your
hands really externally rotated.

01:11:18.050 --> 01:11:19.050
Surprisingly hard to do.

01:11:19.050 --> 01:11:24.680
There are people who can do 20, 30 dips who
cannot do two proper gymnastics ring dips.

01:11:24.680 --> 01:11:29.600
So if you can only do two or three, you’re
doing a max effort set, effectively.

01:11:29.600 --> 01:11:35.100
It makes it hard to get the skill practice
of that movement.

01:11:35.100 --> 01:11:41.280
So you could use something like a 50/50 to
allow you to do maybe 10, 15 total reps; 20

01:11:41.280 --> 01:11:42.810
in a workout.

01:11:42.810 --> 01:11:47.949
The handstand rig that I was trying to figure
out came out of a problem.

01:11:47.949 --> 01:11:53.120
And the problem was if I were kicking up to
a wall, or facing a wall, and then losing

01:11:53.120 --> 01:11:54.360
my balance and coming out of it –

01:11:54.360 --> 01:11:55.580
Jason Nemer: Even worse.

01:11:55.580 --> 01:11:56.580
Tim Ferriss: Exactly.

01:11:56.580 --> 01:12:01.679
My total time in the handstand position for
practicing the alignment, tightness and balance

01:12:01.679 --> 01:12:02.969
was really minimal.

01:12:02.969 --> 01:12:06.040
It was kind of like surfing, where it’s
like okay, I want to get good at surfing,

01:12:06.040 --> 01:12:08.360
meaning standing on the board and carving.

01:12:08.360 --> 01:12:10.490
But 99 percent of your time is spent paddling.

01:12:10.490 --> 01:12:11.490
Jason Nemer: Swimming!

01:12:11.490 --> 01:12:12.540
Tim Ferriss: Yes, swimming and paddling.

01:12:12.540 --> 01:12:14.930
That’s fine; it’s part of the sport.

01:12:14.930 --> 01:12:19.510
But what if you could use a wave pool or what
if you could use wake surfing?

01:12:19.510 --> 01:12:25.310
Suddenly, you’re able to really accelerate
your progress in some very interesting ways.

01:12:25.310 --> 01:12:26.330
We were chatting a little bit earlier.

01:12:26.330 --> 01:12:29.470
I didn’t tell you about the rig but you
were like, does it involve just touching your

01:12:29.470 --> 01:12:31.060
toes on something?

01:12:31.060 --> 01:12:33.960
Which is what Coach Sommer calls a Chinese
handstand.

01:12:33.960 --> 01:12:37.330
So you’re underneath a bar of some type
and you’re just touching with the tippy

01:12:37.330 --> 01:12:41.699
tips of your toes, which requires you to maintain
a lot of extension.

01:12:41.699 --> 01:12:46.980
The issue, though, that I would have with
that as a novice is that if I lose the extension

01:12:46.980 --> 01:12:48.630
for a split second, I’m fucked.

01:12:48.630 --> 01:12:50.280
Jason Nemer: You’re in trouble.

01:12:50.280 --> 01:12:52.850
Tim Ferriss: I’m going to fall, one way
or the other.

01:12:52.850 --> 01:12:53.850
Jason Nemer: Gravity is going to win.

01:12:53.850 --> 01:12:57.290
Tim Ferriss: And then I have to get the hell
back up there, which is very, very nuanced.

01:12:57.290 --> 01:13:01.210
So what I realized is I have a squat rack,
it’s a Rogue rack.

01:13:01.210 --> 01:13:04.969
There’s a dip attachment that goes on the
side.

01:13:04.969 --> 01:13:08.909
The dip attachment is literally about this
big.

01:13:08.909 --> 01:13:12.480
What I’m doing, for those people who can’t
see me, my arms are bent 90 degrees, elbows

01:13:12.480 --> 01:13:17.159
at the sides and my hands are straight out,
kind of like a Lego figure.

01:13:17.159 --> 01:13:20.420
It’s roughly this big.

01:13:20.420 --> 01:13:30.250
What I realized is the actual grips were big
enough for an Olympic weight plate collar.

01:13:30.250 --> 01:13:32.180
I had some Therabands.

01:13:32.180 --> 01:13:36.409
So I could put a Theraband around the very
end and then lock them on with collars.

01:13:36.409 --> 01:13:44.020
I could kick up, get my feet inside, and then
that allows me to be that one bone, one body

01:13:44.020 --> 01:13:47.860
and use my wrists and my shoulders to correct.

01:13:47.860 --> 01:13:49.810
As I get fatigued, of course, I’m going
to make mistakes.

01:13:49.810 --> 01:13:55.150
I’m going to start correcting by piking
and doing all these bad things that I’ll

01:13:55.150 --> 01:14:00.250
get chastised for later by Coach Sommer in
some hilarious emails that I hope to publish

01:14:00.250 --> 01:14:01.250
at some point.

01:14:01.250 --> 01:14:03.530
Jason Nemer: The Sommer diaries.

01:14:03.530 --> 01:14:06.080
Tim Ferriss: The Sommer diaries.

01:14:06.080 --> 01:14:09.370
The Notebook, featuring Coach Sommer and Tim
Ferris.

01:14:09.370 --> 01:14:12.140
You just tuck down and then you’re out.

01:14:12.140 --> 01:14:18.850
It’s just been an incredible accelerator
in terms of getting just mileage in, in that

01:14:18.850 --> 01:14:20.200
proper position.

01:14:20.200 --> 01:14:26.139
Jason Nemer: This doesn’t surprise me because
in the year plus that I’ve known you, you’ve

01:14:26.139 --> 01:14:32.370
asked me so many seemingly gentle questions
that have rocked my world.

01:14:32.370 --> 01:14:37.409
And one of them that you asked recently was,
is there a sequence that basically puts together

01:14:37.409 --> 01:14:40.750
all of the important poses in AcroYoga?

01:14:40.750 --> 01:14:42.050
And I don’t know if you know Bolero?

01:14:42.050 --> 01:14:43.050
Tim Ferriss: I don’t.

01:14:43.050 --> 01:14:44.120
Jason Nemer: It’s a classical song.

01:14:44.120 --> 01:14:45.469
Tim Ferriss: Oh, I think I do.

01:14:45.469 --> 01:14:46.699
Is Bolero the one that’s played in Ten?

01:14:46.699 --> 01:14:51.560
It’s the one that’s played in Ten with
Dudley Moore and Bo Derek.

01:14:51.560 --> 01:14:52.830
Jason Nemer: Yes.

01:14:52.830 --> 01:14:54.220
And also Caveman, if you’ve seen that movie.

01:14:54.220 --> 01:14:59.730
But basically, the guy who wrote that – I
think it’s Ravel – he wrote that to educate

01:14:59.730 --> 01:15:02.710
people in all the different pieces of the
orchestra.

01:15:02.710 --> 01:15:03.710
Tim Ferriss: Oh, cool!

01:15:03.710 --> 01:15:07.870
Jason Nemer: So it starts with violin and
viola, and it’s actually a beautiful song.

01:15:07.870 --> 01:15:13.750
But I thought, okay, I have to make this sequence
that basically strings together every pose

01:15:13.750 --> 01:15:15.260
that I can think of in AcroYoga.

01:15:15.260 --> 01:15:18.830
I want to put Bolero in the background just
as a very educational thing.

01:15:18.830 --> 01:15:19.980
It is going to be beautiful.

01:15:19.980 --> 01:15:21.480
It’s not about the sequence.

01:15:21.480 --> 01:15:26.280
And basically what I’ve done is I’ve made
these grids with front plank, reverse front

01:15:26.280 --> 01:15:30.650
plank, back plank, all of these different
things in one column and then all at the top.

01:15:30.650 --> 01:15:31.650
And then I cross reference.

01:15:31.650 --> 01:15:34.500
What is it like to go from a throne to a reverse
front plank?

01:15:34.500 --> 01:15:39.150
Is it beginner, intermediate, advanced, or
an expert or not possible?

01:15:39.150 --> 01:15:44.230
So basically what I want to do is go in a
lab, which is with me and a friend, and just

01:15:44.230 --> 01:15:51.000
do all these different transitions and then
basically try to get a unified feel of theory

01:15:51.000 --> 01:15:52.110
of AcroYoga, basically.

01:15:52.110 --> 01:15:56.310
Einstein is one of my heroes and he worked
on this most of the end of his life.

01:15:56.310 --> 01:16:01.530
He was trying to see how the strong nuclear
force, the weak nuclear force, gravity, and

01:16:01.530 --> 01:16:03.699
electromagnetism were all related.

01:16:03.699 --> 01:16:08.290
I feel like something that I want to do with
AcroYoga in my life is to keep pulling out

01:16:08.290 --> 01:16:15.010
these equations, pulling out these similarities
between these seemingly unrelated things that

01:16:15.010 --> 01:16:19.349
once you crack the code – and this is very
Tim Ferris because you asked the question

01:16:19.349 --> 01:16:23.739
– once you crack the code, if I can come
up with a five-minute sequence that has all

01:16:23.739 --> 01:16:26.389
of these very important, basic moves.

01:16:26.389 --> 01:16:31.170
That’s what unlocks the human potential
for very complicated flying transitions.

01:16:31.170 --> 01:16:35.830
Tim Ferriss: It also provides, well, No. 1,
I can’t wait to try it.

01:16:35.830 --> 01:16:39.449
No. 2, it also provides motivation for the
student.

01:16:39.449 --> 01:16:44.310
And I think that if you look at, for instance
– and this is part of where the thinking

01:16:44.310 --> 01:16:49.880
came from because the frustration was born
of – and frustration, I think, in many cases

01:16:49.880 --> 01:16:51.810
is a good thing.

01:16:51.810 --> 01:16:55.060
It gives you an itch that needs to be scratched
and you need to figure out how to scratch

01:16:55.060 --> 01:16:56.060
it.

01:16:56.060 --> 01:17:05.290
In this particular case, in any physical practice
that is not regulated in some way by high

01:17:05.290 --> 01:17:09.640
stakes competition, which has its own kind
of filtering and self-selection, but it’s

01:17:09.640 --> 01:17:10.640
totally different.

01:17:10.640 --> 01:17:15.060
Because let’s say you’re in China and
you’re putting together a national gymnastics

01:17:15.060 --> 01:17:16.060
team.

01:17:16.060 --> 01:17:21.080
You don’t care if you completely destroy
999 athletes to find the one who can tolerate

01:17:21.080 --> 01:17:25.390
just an inhuman amount of workload and has
freakish joints.

01:17:25.390 --> 01:17:27.159
Jason Nemer: That’s how the Chinese train.

01:17:27.159 --> 01:17:28.159
Tim Ferriss: Exactly.

01:17:28.159 --> 01:17:30.710
And it’s also how a lot of military train
dogs, for instance.

01:17:30.710 --> 01:17:36.730
They don’t care if 99 of the dogs get completely
messed up as long as they find the one that’s

01:17:36.730 --> 01:17:39.630
going to save 100 lives in the field or whatever
it might be.

01:17:39.630 --> 01:17:44.750
I shouldn’t say they don’t care but it’s
just an unfortunate common side effect.

01:17:44.750 --> 01:17:48.780
But when you’re dealing with recreational
practitioners, and you don’t have, say,

01:17:48.780 --> 01:17:53.630
a national championship and that vetting process
and anyone who wants to participate can participate,

01:17:53.630 --> 01:17:58.900
I think it’s more akin in some ways to child’s
martial arts.

01:17:58.900 --> 01:18:01.480
So how do you keep kids interested in martial
arts?

01:18:01.480 --> 01:18:02.480
You have belts.

01:18:02.480 --> 01:18:03.920
How do you get the belt?

01:18:03.920 --> 01:18:09.230
You have a kata, you have a set form that
at least in theory incorporates all of the

01:18:09.230 --> 01:18:15.610
requisite skills that would represent that
level of competence or mastery.

01:18:15.610 --> 01:18:18.320
Another analogy would be skiing slopes.

01:18:18.320 --> 01:18:24.900
So you have the green circle, the blue square
– I might be getting this wrong – and

01:18:24.900 --> 01:18:31.030
then the black diamond, and those are based
on the characteristics of the slope; the steepness,

01:18:31.030 --> 01:18:35.520
the terrain features, moguls and so on.

01:18:35.520 --> 01:18:39.880
So I was thinking to myself, would it be possible
– and this would also help the business

01:18:39.880 --> 01:18:44.250
or the organization of AcroYoga in terms of
retention.

01:18:44.250 --> 01:18:51.640
If people want to get to the next belt, i.e.
master the next five-minute sequence, it’s

01:18:51.640 --> 01:18:57.770
a mutually beneficial construct because it
keeps the students motivated and it keeps

01:18:57.770 --> 01:19:00.260
the entire ecosystem thriving.

01:19:00.260 --> 01:19:08.250
I remember thinking to myself, if you look
at whether it’s jiu-jitsu or judo or AcroYoga,

01:19:08.250 --> 01:19:09.250
it’s very common.

01:19:09.250 --> 01:19:13.500
You go into a class and it’s whatever the
teacher might want to work on that day, or

01:19:13.500 --> 01:19:16.022
whatever occurred to them on the drive over;
maybe it’s something they want to practice

01:19:16.022 --> 01:19:17.050
themselves.

01:19:17.050 --> 01:19:20.140
And there’s a lot of benefit to that.

01:19:20.140 --> 01:19:30.100
But at least for an OCD perfectionist idiot
like myself, I need to know where I’m aiming.

01:19:30.100 --> 01:19:31.100
Jason Nemer: Where you’re going, yeah.

01:19:31.100 --> 01:19:34.390
Tim Ferriss: And so the idea of the sequence
was really fun.

01:19:34.390 --> 01:19:37.440
That’s what, as you know, I’ve been working
on for the last eight weeks or so.

01:19:37.440 --> 01:19:42.200
And ended up getting the entire sequence,
except for one which everybody had trouble

01:19:42.200 --> 01:19:45.199
with, even the teachers so I don’t feel
that badly about it; the London spin.

01:19:45.199 --> 01:19:46.830
Jason Nemer: Yeah, that one’s hard.

01:19:46.830 --> 01:19:48.179
Tim Ferriss: That’s a motherfucker.

01:19:48.179 --> 01:19:51.560
But we were able to do it 80 percent of the
time.

01:19:51.560 --> 01:19:52.900
But it ain’t pretty.

01:19:52.900 --> 01:19:57.420
But the rest of it, it was really fantastic
to have.

01:19:57.420 --> 01:20:02.580
This is something that Josh Waitzkin who’s
been on the podcast, so the inspiration for

01:20:02.580 --> 01:20:05.480
the book and the movie, Searching for Bobby
Fisher.

01:20:05.480 --> 01:20:09.510
Considered a chess prodigy but I don’t think
that applies, even though he has incredible

01:20:09.510 --> 01:20:10.520
talents.

01:20:10.520 --> 01:20:14.740
He can take his learning system, his approach,
and apply it to just about anything; jiu-jitsu,

01:20:14.740 --> 01:20:17.960
his first black belt under Marcelo Garcia
who’s like the Michael Jordan of jiu-jitsu,

01:20:17.960 --> 01:20:20.070
ten time world champion, I don’t know.

01:20:20.070 --> 01:20:22.530
Just there’s no close second place.

01:20:22.530 --> 01:20:26.790
He’s also applied it to a number of other
fields: tai chi, and he’s now applied it

01:20:26.790 --> 01:20:29.350
to surfing.

01:20:29.350 --> 01:20:31.930
And he would call it learning the macro from
the micro.

01:20:31.930 --> 01:20:34.060
Jason Nemer: Oh, I like that a lot.

01:20:34.060 --> 01:20:39.890
Tim Ferriss: So for instance, and you can
learn a lot about someone’s body awareness

01:20:39.890 --> 01:20:42.300
just by having them do an ATB drill, right?

01:20:42.300 --> 01:20:43.300
Jason Nemer: Sure.

01:20:43.300 --> 01:20:49.449
Tim Ferriss: If you want to learn, let’s
say, how someone is in language, well, you

01:20:49.449 --> 01:20:56.230
could just offer them two pages of dialogue
that include idiomatic jokes, humor.

01:20:56.230 --> 01:20:57.860
That’s a high bar.

01:20:57.860 --> 01:21:01.250
You can figure out a lot by seeing how they
interact with foreign language humor.

01:21:01.250 --> 01:21:05.719
And in Acro, like the corkscrew, that’s
a really good diagnostic tool.

01:21:05.719 --> 01:21:07.050
You have to do a lot in the corkscrew.

01:21:07.050 --> 01:21:11.450
Jason Nemer: You have to have a lot of proprioceptive
ability and the thing that’s unique with

01:21:11.450 --> 01:21:16.020
AcroYoga is you’ve got two people or three
people.

01:21:16.020 --> 01:21:20.469
One of my definitions of an advanced practitioner
is somebody who can have success with anybody.

01:21:20.469 --> 01:21:26.040
And my first master teacher I ever saw was
a Bulgarian guy named Dema Tarmentoff.

01:21:26.040 --> 01:21:30.909
He would literally pick up a 6-year-old kid,
hold him in his hands, chuck him up in the

01:21:30.909 --> 01:21:34.160
air and do this thing called a dislocate,
push him to a high hand to hand and hold them

01:21:34.160 --> 01:21:35.710
in a one-arm handstand.

01:21:35.710 --> 01:21:41.400
I saw this when I was 12, 13 and I was like,
yeah, I want to get that good.

01:21:41.400 --> 01:21:45.000
The ability to really support any different
person –

01:21:45.000 --> 01:21:47.350
Tim Ferriss: Dislocate sounds terrible.

01:21:47.350 --> 01:21:50.170
Is that where you come here and they jump
through the middle?

01:21:50.170 --> 01:21:51.210
Jason Nemer: That’s inlocate.

01:21:51.210 --> 01:21:52.520
Tim Ferriss: That’s inlocate, okay.

01:21:52.520 --> 01:21:53.540
So dislocate is the other way.

01:21:53.540 --> 01:21:56.130
Jason Nemer: Dislocate is the other way, where
the arms are here and they go like that.

01:21:56.130 --> 01:21:57.130
Tim Ferriss: Oh!

01:21:57.130 --> 01:21:58.130
Jason Nemer: Yeah, you’ve got to have some
mobility for that.

01:21:58.130 --> 01:21:59.130
Tim Ferriss: I need to work on that.

01:21:59.130 --> 01:22:00.130
I’m not ready.

01:22:00.130 --> 01:22:01.130
Jason Nemer: You are never doing that.

01:22:01.130 --> 01:22:02.780
You can base it; you’re never flying in
it.

01:22:02.780 --> 01:22:05.440
I don’t say never that often.

01:22:05.440 --> 01:22:07.480
Tim Ferriss: I’m happy to save my shoulders.

01:22:07.480 --> 01:22:09.090
They’ve had enough abuse.

01:22:09.090 --> 01:22:16.880
Left shoulder already has pins in it; I don’t
need more of those.

01:22:16.880 --> 01:22:21.990
I really implore people, and you can find,
I’m sure, a synopsis on this online, but

01:22:21.990 --> 01:22:25.280
get a hold of the meta learning section of
the Four-Hour Chef because it talks about

01:22:25.280 --> 01:22:30.760
how to work on one skill, to develop it in
such a way that it transfers to many other

01:22:30.760 --> 01:22:31.760
skills.

01:22:31.760 --> 01:22:36.290
So for instance, if we’re looking at sequencing
and role switching, I think this is really

01:22:36.290 --> 01:22:38.310
helpful for any type of partner practice.

01:22:38.310 --> 01:22:43.370
In the world of tango, when I was in Argentina,
I learned the female role before I learned

01:22:43.370 --> 01:22:44.370
the male role.

01:22:44.370 --> 01:22:45.370
Jason Nemer: It’s so much easier to dance.

01:22:45.370 --> 01:22:46.440
Tim Ferriss: That seems really weird.

01:22:46.440 --> 01:22:52.730
But working with Alicia Monte, who is an incredible
world-class dancer, learning the female role

01:22:52.730 --> 01:22:54.820
because we had to; we didn’t have enough
women in the class.

01:22:54.820 --> 01:22:56.260
That’s how it came about.

01:22:56.260 --> 01:23:00.230
It’s like hey, when Buenos Aries was being
populated by immigrants, the dudes would dance

01:23:00.230 --> 01:23:03.770
with one another and it’s like hey, this
isn’t just for the Castro.

01:23:03.770 --> 01:23:07.330
There’s actually a functional purpose, here.

01:23:07.330 --> 01:23:12.330
After learning the female role, it made it
infinitely easier for me to learn the male

01:23:12.330 --> 01:23:13.330
role.

01:23:13.330 --> 01:23:17.160
Now in Acro, it’s not a strictly male-female
thing, as we just saw.

01:23:17.160 --> 01:23:21.400
But very often, because men are sometimes
or often bigger than the women, they end up

01:23:21.400 --> 01:23:22.400
basing.

01:23:22.400 --> 01:23:24.340
And they’re just not very flexible, often
times.

01:23:24.340 --> 01:23:25.340
You are.

01:23:25.340 --> 01:23:32.580
But guys will be like, I have strong legs;
I’ll just hold you on my legs.

01:23:32.580 --> 01:23:38.810
Even though I’m kind of chunky by Acro standards,
as a flyer I try to fly whenever possible

01:23:38.810 --> 01:23:43.960
when there’s someone like you, or Justin
Bentz, or Daniel Scott or some of these guys

01:23:43.960 --> 01:23:46.820
who are just incredibly solid bases.

01:23:46.820 --> 01:23:49.949
I want to fly because it helps my ability
to base.

01:23:49.949 --> 01:23:55.100
Jason Nemer: Also, one step further than just
understanding, is compassion.

01:23:55.100 --> 01:23:59.810
If you’re always basing and you’re saying
to the flyers, why are you so scared?

01:23:59.810 --> 01:24:03.100
Then you fly, and you’re like oh, my God,
this shit is scary.

01:24:03.100 --> 01:24:06.480
Or vice versa if you’re the flyer and you
base a little bit, you’re like, why are

01:24:06.480 --> 01:24:07.480
you so wobbly?

01:24:07.480 --> 01:24:10.389
Then you try it and oh, I understand why you’re
wobbly, it’s hard.

01:24:10.389 --> 01:24:18.850
Tim Ferriss: In South America there’s this
one woman, very nice, very super flexy flyer,

01:24:18.850 --> 01:24:22.900
who was – and I take it that this is a common
term – bossy flyer?

01:24:22.900 --> 01:24:24.219
Jason Nemer: Yeah.

01:24:24.219 --> 01:24:28.760
Tim Ferriss: Just like berating the shit out
of every other base.

01:24:28.760 --> 01:24:30.710
It was just like okay, why don’t you try
it?

01:24:30.710 --> 01:24:31.710
We were doing hand to hands.

01:24:31.710 --> 01:24:33.570
It’s like, why don’t you try it?

01:24:33.570 --> 01:24:34.710
Why don’t you try to base?

01:24:34.710 --> 01:24:36.100
Jason Nemer: That was a humble pie.

01:24:36.100 --> 01:24:38.930
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, and that remedied the situation
pretty quickly.

01:24:38.930 --> 01:24:41.720
And it’s not that one is harder than the
other.

01:24:41.720 --> 01:24:49.449
At any respectable level, they both involve
enough complexity and physical demand to be

01:24:49.449 --> 01:24:55.230
a humdinger, I think, and very humbling.

01:24:55.230 --> 01:24:59.500
For people who are listening or watching,
we’ve talked about the acrobatic flying,

01:24:59.500 --> 01:25:02.010
we’ve talked about the therapeutic flying.

01:25:02.010 --> 01:25:06.219
The most interesting is not treating those
two as separate practices.

01:25:06.219 --> 01:25:10.550
But in the case of, for instance, when I’m
practicing now when I’m doing say 45 minutes

01:25:10.550 --> 01:25:14.219
of acrobatic and then 15 minutes therapeutic.

01:25:14.219 --> 01:25:19.130
For me, it’s like having two extra days
of recovery.

01:25:19.130 --> 01:25:23.710
It was reminiscent, in a way, to what I used
to experience when I was training at this

01:25:23.710 --> 01:25:26.909
place called Fairtex here in San Francisco
for Muay Thai.

01:25:26.909 --> 01:25:37.780
The Thai trainers would do very rough but
nonetheless therapeutic Thai massage.

01:25:37.780 --> 01:25:40.500
Technically they were Thai and they were massaging
me, so I guess it was Thai massage.

01:25:40.500 --> 01:25:44.320
But they would walk on you before and after.

01:25:44.320 --> 01:25:48.770
That was just part of the warm-up and the
cool-down.

01:25:48.770 --> 01:25:56.139
This just reminded me of that self care, what
people might dismiss if they’re kind of

01:25:56.139 --> 01:26:02.580
hard edged males especially, as too soft and
new agey or whatever, is actually a performance

01:26:02.580 --> 01:26:05.989
enhancer for the hard aspects, in part.

01:26:05.989 --> 01:26:07.179
Jason Nemer: For sure.

01:26:07.179 --> 01:26:12.190
And this is the term hatha yoga, a lot of
people hear it; it means sun and moon.

01:26:12.190 --> 01:26:13.750
Ha is sun, tha is moon.

01:26:13.750 --> 01:26:14.750
Tim Ferriss: I didn’t know that.

01:26:14.750 --> 01:26:19.040
Jason Nemer: A lot of the physical practice
of yoga is about getting the masculine and

01:26:19.040 --> 01:26:20.520
the feminine energy to balance.

01:26:20.520 --> 01:26:27.020
So if you are on either side of the spectrum,
we are all on one side of the other unless

01:26:27.020 --> 01:26:29.560
we’re super balanced between strength and
flexibility.

01:26:29.560 --> 01:26:33.180
Some people naturally are more strong; some
people naturally are more flexible.

01:26:33.180 --> 01:26:38.500
Wherever you are as an athlete, as an acrobat,
and even as a yogi, my belief is that you

01:26:38.500 --> 01:26:40.190
want to get towards that 50/50.

01:26:40.190 --> 01:26:46.130
So somebody like you is not going to expand
their AcroYoga or their yoga practice as intelligently

01:26:46.130 --> 01:26:48.180
until you get more mobility.

01:26:48.180 --> 01:26:54.801
And so being able to put in these therapeutic
poses at the end of a practice, it’s not

01:26:54.801 --> 01:26:59.080
just to make your body feel better in that
moment; it’s to set you up better for your

01:26:59.080 --> 01:27:00.080
next practice.

01:27:00.080 --> 01:27:03.610
Because if you’re building strength and
you’re not building range of motion, you’re

01:27:03.610 --> 01:27:04.880
basically making a time bomb.

01:27:04.880 --> 01:27:08.190
And when things go off, then you really need
some deep healing.

01:27:08.190 --> 01:27:13.090
So if you do homeopathic doses of healing
along the way, unwinding the tension that

01:27:13.090 --> 01:27:15.860
you’ve built up in your practice, it’s
just intelligent training.

01:27:15.860 --> 01:27:17.630
Tim Ferriss: The Princess Bride method?

01:27:17.630 --> 01:27:18.679
Iocane powder?

01:27:18.679 --> 01:27:20.250
Jason Nemer: Exactly.

01:27:20.250 --> 01:27:21.450
Did you put it in?

01:27:21.450 --> 01:27:25.000
Tim Ferriss: The Dread Pirate Roberts’ training
curriculum.

01:27:25.000 --> 01:27:27.429
Jason Nemer: Inconceivable!

01:27:27.429 --> 01:27:30.660
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, exactly.

01:27:30.660 --> 01:27:36.880
The simplest perhaps incentive that I had
very early on to base, because I was watching

01:27:36.880 --> 01:27:38.410
and I’m like, that’s cool.

01:27:38.410 --> 01:27:40.740
I think that would be really helpful for jiu-jitsu.

01:27:40.740 --> 01:27:49.679
And in fact, there’s a fair amount of crossover,
people who have good kind of rubbery guards

01:27:49.679 --> 01:27:51.110
are really effective bases.

01:27:51.110 --> 01:27:53.850
And if they want to improve one or the other,
they’re very symbiotic.

01:27:53.850 --> 01:27:54.850
Jason Nemer: Right.

01:27:54.850 --> 01:28:00.699
Tim Ferriss: But what I realized very quickly
after my first or second attempt, because

01:28:00.699 --> 01:28:04.590
I was like, it looks cool, the flying looks
like a little bit more fun but I’m too chunky,

01:28:04.590 --> 01:28:06.530
and the basing looks like a lot of work.

01:28:06.530 --> 01:28:10.960
But then I did it and what I realized was
when we’re sitting down like this – so

01:28:10.960 --> 01:28:14.420
I had developed some back and hip issues – we’re
sitting like this a lot.

01:28:14.420 --> 01:28:18.950
And I’m sure Kelly Starrett, PT extraordinaire
could comment on this more intelligently,

01:28:18.950 --> 01:28:22.130
but you’re effectively pushing the head
of your femur forward.

01:28:22.130 --> 01:28:28.520
You’re sitting down, your femur is getting
set or pressured in an unnatural direction

01:28:28.520 --> 01:28:30.100
over extended periods of time.

01:28:30.100 --> 01:28:34.900
When I lay on my back, put my legs straight
up in the air and support weight, I’m reseating

01:28:34.900 --> 01:28:39.280
the femur in the proper place.

01:28:39.280 --> 01:28:43.130
That alone, I feel like just basing someone,
even if it was just basing someone in folded

01:28:43.130 --> 01:28:49.670
leaf or bird, which we did – Jack, I’m
flying kind of thing earlier – for a few

01:28:49.670 --> 01:28:55.239
minutes at the end of the day is just incredibly
beneficial.

01:28:55.239 --> 01:28:59.969
Let me ask some rapid fire questions, the
usual.

01:28:59.969 --> 01:29:06.440
When you think of the word successful, who
is the first person who comes to mind, or

01:29:06.440 --> 01:29:07.440
people?

01:29:07.440 --> 01:29:15.739
Jason Nemer: I’ve got a tie: Gandhi, super
successful, and Einstein; from very different

01:29:15.739 --> 01:29:17.340
spectrums of humanity.

01:29:17.340 --> 01:29:20.350
They actually met in South America when they
were both alive.

01:29:20.350 --> 01:29:23.949
I would have loved to have been the fly on
the wall for that one.

01:29:23.949 --> 01:29:30.300
What I see in Einstein that I really appreciate
is he had all the proof he needed to just

01:29:30.300 --> 01:29:34.040
keep moving forward with the way that people
understood the world.

01:29:34.040 --> 01:29:37.500
And he basically stopped listening to everybody.

01:29:37.500 --> 01:29:39.290
He really went inside.

01:29:39.290 --> 01:29:45.909
He thought about things, he worked them out,
and he changed the world just from his mind.

01:29:45.909 --> 01:29:51.190
The ability to be self reliant, to believe
in yourself and to change the world because

01:29:51.190 --> 01:29:57.390
of how brilliant you are and how open your
mind is, that’s a high bar.

01:29:57.390 --> 01:30:04.369
I’ve read more about his life and he writes
about love beautifully.

01:30:04.369 --> 01:30:08.469
One of my favorite quotes of his, I’m not
going to say it right because it’s in German,

01:30:08.469 --> 01:30:12.510
he says, “I like to cut wood because I can
see the fruits of my labor.”

01:30:12.510 --> 01:30:16.290
It’s just a lot of really simple things,
because as much as he was in the stars, he

01:30:16.290 --> 01:30:18.160
was very pragmatic.

01:30:18.160 --> 01:30:25.300
Gandhi, similarly, he didn’t see the world
in the box that he was born into.

01:30:25.300 --> 01:30:30.630
When I see people that really don’t take
the world as it’s being presented, and they

01:30:30.630 --> 01:30:34.880
have a vision of how things can be and just
that resolve, I feel like both of them were

01:30:34.880 --> 01:30:39.650
so committed to their vision and had so much
resolve, and both of them changed the world

01:30:39.650 --> 01:30:41.199
in such big ways.

01:30:41.199 --> 01:30:45.170
That’s a big definition of success for me.

01:30:45.170 --> 01:30:48.790
Tim Ferriss: I want to highlight something
you just said.

01:30:48.790 --> 01:30:55.949
Because the ability of Einstein to comment
on beauty, is something that I think – or

01:30:55.949 --> 01:31:07.199
to speak on the qualitative aspects of life
with the razor sharp perception of a trained

01:31:07.199 --> 01:31:13.210
scientist is something that’s underappreciated,
I think, by people who might be inclined to

01:31:13.210 --> 01:31:14.210
be scientists.

01:31:14.210 --> 01:31:15.900
It’s very clinical and dry.

01:31:15.900 --> 01:31:21.940
Richard Feynman, another great example, physicist
and phenomenal teacher.

01:31:21.940 --> 01:31:28.590
Surely You’re Joking, Mr. Feynman! is one
of my favorite books of all time.

01:31:28.590 --> 01:31:31.830
Do you have any particular books you’ve
gifted most to other people?

01:31:31.830 --> 01:31:33.869
Jason Nemer: The Prophet.

01:31:33.869 --> 01:31:37.030
Tim Ferriss: The Prophet.

01:31:37.030 --> 01:31:38.679
These are lessons from Jesus?

01:31:38.679 --> 01:31:41.699
Jason Nemer: No, he’s a Lebanese guy.

01:31:41.699 --> 01:31:46.139
I don’t remember exactly his name, it’s
with a K; first name with a K, last name with

01:31:46.139 --> 01:31:48.150
a G. I cannot pronounce it.

01:31:48.150 --> 01:31:50.510
Tim Ferriss: That’s alright.

01:31:50.510 --> 01:31:54.980
Jason Nemer: He’s this guy who is speaking
to a crowd.

01:31:54.980 --> 01:31:58.280
And somebody in the crowd will say, speak
to us of pain.

01:31:58.280 --> 01:32:02.219
And he’ll say pain is the blah, blah, blah.

01:32:02.219 --> 01:32:05.670
Speak to me of love, speak to me of being
a father.

01:32:05.670 --> 01:32:12.600
And I love really – the hairs are standing
up as I think about it – I love really accurate,

01:32:12.600 --> 01:32:21.210
condense, Shakti field, energy field statements;
something that you can read in a few minutes

01:32:21.210 --> 01:32:22.750
or you can read for your whole life.

01:32:22.750 --> 01:32:27.520
So the Tao Te Ching would be a close second,
and I feel like they have similar things where

01:32:27.520 --> 01:32:33.570
there’s so much energy in each passage that
you can sit down and speed read it but the

01:32:33.570 --> 01:32:39.530
depth of these ideas and these concepts, that’s
actually the book that I have on my traveling

01:32:39.530 --> 01:32:40.780
altar because all I do is travel.

01:32:40.780 --> 01:32:45.590
So I have the Tao Te Ching and oftentimes
before meditation, I’ll just open it randomly

01:32:45.590 --> 01:32:50.440
to a page and read about something and then
just have that be what I steep in as I sit.

01:32:50.440 --> 01:32:54.600
Tim Ferriss: I have to ask because you just
reminded me of it.

01:32:54.600 --> 01:32:56.020
You are constantly traveling.

01:32:56.020 --> 01:32:57.830
You have a very nomadic existence.

01:32:57.830 --> 01:32:58.830
Jason Nemer: Not very.

01:32:58.830 --> 01:33:00.400
I am fully nomadic.

01:33:00.400 --> 01:33:03.570
Tim Ferriss: What belongings do you travel
with?

01:33:03.570 --> 01:33:07.619
Jason Nemer: I have a rolling suitcase, a
backpack, and an ukulele.

01:33:07.619 --> 01:33:11.699
In the backpack I have my computer, a bunch
of notes that are going to turn into a book

01:33:11.699 --> 01:33:14.330
sometime probably this year, and it’s really
dangerous.

01:33:14.330 --> 01:33:15.330
Tim Ferriss: Nonfiction?

01:33:15.330 --> 01:33:18.909
Jason Nemer: It’s AcroYoga stuff.

01:33:18.909 --> 01:33:20.630
I have my altar items in my backpack.

01:33:20.630 --> 01:33:22.340
Tim Ferriss: What are in your altar items?

01:33:22.340 --> 01:33:26.600
Jason Nemer: Different things from different
places, things that I’ve been gifted from

01:33:26.600 --> 01:33:27.600
students.

01:33:27.600 --> 01:33:28.600
Tim Ferriss: Like what?

01:33:28.600 --> 01:33:31.260
Jason Nemer: Rocks, I got some earrings from
a girl that I’m her godfather.

01:33:31.260 --> 01:33:34.600
I’ve got a Mexican flag.

01:33:34.600 --> 01:33:40.430
The box is actually from Lebanon because I
was born in Mexico but I have Lebanese heritage.

01:33:40.430 --> 01:33:43.210
So it’s just as lot of mementos that remind
me, some photos, as well.

01:33:43.210 --> 01:33:47.500
I have a photo of Dharma Mittra, I have a
photo of myself when I was in China competing

01:33:47.500 --> 01:33:49.290
at world championships when I was 16.

01:33:49.290 --> 01:33:54.060
It’s just things that feel important to
me that keep me grounded.

01:33:54.060 --> 01:33:59.239
Tim Ferriss: So in a sense, would you say
– and I don’t want to put words in your

01:33:59.239 --> 01:34:01.600
mouth, but I was going to ask, what is the
function of the altar?

01:34:01.600 --> 01:34:06.510
But is it your mobile sense of home?

01:34:06.510 --> 01:34:07.860
Is that effectively what it is?

01:34:07.860 --> 01:34:08.910
Jason Nemer: Yeah.

01:34:08.910 --> 01:34:13.600
To a large degree, because of my life and
lifestyle, my home is my community and I have

01:34:13.600 --> 01:34:17.970
friends all over the world that if and when
– it’s a when; it’s not an if – when

01:34:17.970 --> 01:34:22.449
I stop traveling full-time, I’m going to
not have that part of my life anymore.

01:34:22.449 --> 01:34:26.850
So to be able to go to Germany and hang out
with Julie and Pascal and their daughter Leah,

01:34:26.850 --> 01:34:31.500
who I love to death and I’ve seen the baby
since she was in the belly and done acrobatics

01:34:31.500 --> 01:34:34.949
with them for many, many years; that’s really
what home is.

01:34:34.949 --> 01:34:39.199
But having the altar, the unpacking the altar
ritual is just…

01:34:39.199 --> 01:34:43.600
When I was a kid, I had Star Wars toys and
games and I would love to set up my Star Wars

01:34:43.600 --> 01:34:44.600
stuff.

01:34:44.600 --> 01:34:46.530
It’s the same thing, where it’s like this
is my magic kit.

01:34:46.530 --> 01:34:49.730
This is what makes me feel like a kid; it
makes me feel excited.

01:34:49.730 --> 01:34:52.790
I put a candle on a lot of times when I’m
meditating.

01:34:52.790 --> 01:34:55.540
It’s my happy place, my traveling happy
place.

01:34:55.540 --> 01:34:57.020
Tim Ferriss: What else do you have?

01:34:57.020 --> 01:34:58.920
Jason Nemer: I have some throwing knives.

01:34:58.920 --> 01:35:00.180
Tim Ferriss: Throwing knives?

01:35:00.180 --> 01:35:02.190
Any particular type of throwing knife?

01:35:02.190 --> 01:35:04.179
Jason Nemer: I’ve had many types.

01:35:04.179 --> 01:35:08.900
Throwing knives are easy to lose so pretty
much every time I roll through Sacramento,

01:35:08.900 --> 01:35:10.810
there’s a place that I know that has them.

01:35:10.810 --> 01:35:12.210
That’s where my family is.

01:35:12.210 --> 01:35:13.429
Tim Ferriss: Can you mention it?

01:35:13.429 --> 01:35:14.429
Jason Nemer: Wild Sports.

01:35:14.429 --> 01:35:15.770
Tim Ferriss: Wild Sports throwing knives.

01:35:15.770 --> 01:35:19.630
Jason Nemer: Yeah, they’ve got guns, they’ve
got worms and they’ve got throwing knives.

01:35:19.630 --> 01:35:22.430
Tim Ferriss: You’re ready for the zombie
apocalypse.

01:35:22.430 --> 01:35:23.640
Jason Nemer: You never know.

01:35:23.640 --> 01:35:26.540
Tim Ferriss: Have you ever been stopped in
customs for the throwing knives?

01:35:26.540 --> 01:35:28.100
Jason Nemer: I haven’t.

01:35:28.100 --> 01:35:31.370
The knives were lifted out of my bag in Panama.

01:35:31.370 --> 01:35:34.750
Tim Ferriss: Lifted meaning permanently borrowed?

01:35:34.750 --> 01:35:36.600
Jason Nemer: Permanently borrowed, yes.

01:35:36.600 --> 01:35:39.800
When I was in Bali, I don’t know if you’ve
been; have you been to Bali?

01:35:39.800 --> 01:35:40.800
Tim Ferriss: I have, yeah.

01:35:40.800 --> 01:35:41.870
Jason Nemer: So you know if you have drugs,
we’re going to kill you?

01:35:41.870 --> 01:35:42.870
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:35:42.870 --> 01:35:47.820
Jason Nemer: Well, another thing on the customs
form is do you have any pointy objects?

01:35:47.820 --> 01:35:50.400
And I was like oh, I really don’t want to
give them up.

01:35:50.400 --> 01:35:52.699
So I smuggled in some throwing knives to Bali.

01:35:52.699 --> 01:35:54.110
I’ve said it out loud.

01:35:54.110 --> 01:35:56.810
The Balinese police might hunt me down.

01:35:56.810 --> 01:35:59.750
Tim Ferriss: The Balinese Mossad are on their
way.

01:35:59.750 --> 01:36:03.060
If so, that’s a huge budget misallocation.

01:36:03.060 --> 01:36:06.740
Nemer is not the biggest threat out there.

01:36:06.740 --> 01:36:07.949
Anything else that comes to mind?

01:36:07.949 --> 01:36:08.949
Jason Nemer: Frisbee.

01:36:08.949 --> 01:36:09.949
I am a Frisbee golfer.

01:36:09.949 --> 01:36:11.800
Tim Ferriss: I saw a photo of that.

01:36:11.800 --> 01:36:14.090
Jason Nemer: I love Frisbee golf.

01:36:14.090 --> 01:36:18.820
Basically, I love training in many different
ways, and hand-eye coordination.

01:36:18.820 --> 01:36:21.400
Tim Ferriss: Do you have any go-to Frisbee
for Frisbee golf?

01:36:21.400 --> 01:36:23.060
I guess it would be disc golf.

01:36:23.060 --> 01:36:26.389
Jason Nemer: It’s not a sport, it’s a
pastime.

01:36:26.389 --> 01:36:30.070
I correct people when they get really serious
because there are people who have caddies.

01:36:30.070 --> 01:36:31.070
Tim Ferriss: What?

01:36:31.070 --> 01:36:32.310
Jason Nemer: For real.

01:36:32.310 --> 01:36:33.540
Those people think it’s a sport.

01:36:33.540 --> 01:36:37.449
It’s a pastime, no matter how hard you work
at it, it’s a piece of plastic and you’re

01:36:37.449 --> 01:36:39.310
throwing it around.

01:36:39.310 --> 01:36:42.119
I like one called the Rock.

01:36:42.119 --> 01:36:43.469
It’s a mid-range disc.

01:36:43.469 --> 01:36:45.590
It’s very even flying.

01:36:45.590 --> 01:36:48.920
Not super good with distance and the T Bird
is my go-to driver.

01:36:48.920 --> 01:36:50.570
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

01:36:50.570 --> 01:36:53.640
So it really is like having a set of clubs?

01:36:53.640 --> 01:36:56.160
Jason Nemer: Yeah, you’ve got your 9 iron,
you’ve got your pitching wedge.

01:36:56.160 --> 01:36:57.820
And the discs fly differently.

01:36:57.820 --> 01:37:01.400
First of all, you have to get a repeatable
swing before you have any success, just like

01:37:01.400 --> 01:37:02.400
golf.

01:37:02.400 --> 01:37:05.760
But once you have a repeatable swing, you
can feel the nuance of this one fades left,

01:37:05.760 --> 01:37:07.850
this one fades right, this one goes straight.

01:37:07.850 --> 01:37:13.869
Tim Ferriss: Is there anything that you’ve
learned in Acro that has translated to helping

01:37:13.869 --> 01:37:16.880
the Frisbee golf or vice versa?

01:37:16.880 --> 01:37:22.560
Jason Nemer: I think every practice that I
do, they are relatable.

01:37:22.560 --> 01:37:27.810
How well I can listen to you with the podcast,
how well I can do any number of things, it’s

01:37:27.810 --> 01:37:33.310
all sensitivity, confidence, clarity, repeatability.

01:37:33.310 --> 01:37:36.480
So I’m always training and I’m always
playing.

01:37:36.480 --> 01:37:40.350
And playing and training, for me, I’ve figured
out a way for them to be synonymous.

01:37:40.350 --> 01:37:43.180
So I don’t work hard at things.

01:37:43.180 --> 01:37:46.619
I do things that I’m passionate about and
I love what I do.

01:37:46.619 --> 01:37:52.659
So everything is related.

01:37:52.659 --> 01:37:58.440
Tim Ferriss: This is a funny question, coming
up on your very minimalist kit that you travel

01:37:58.440 --> 01:37:59.440
with.

01:37:59.440 --> 01:38:05.910
What is the purchase of $100.00 or less that
has had the biggest positive impact on your

01:38:05.910 --> 01:38:09.970
life in the last memorable space of time?

01:38:09.970 --> 01:38:11.170
Jason Nemer: We’ve already got it.

01:38:11.170 --> 01:38:12.420
It’s the Frisbees, for sure.

01:38:12.420 --> 01:38:14.929
I was hanging out with my two brothers and
my dad.

01:38:14.929 --> 01:38:21.400
We decided to drive up the coast and my two
brothers and me, we’ve played Frisbee golf

01:38:21.400 --> 01:38:24.380
our whole lives so we love it, and my dad,
not so much.

01:38:24.380 --> 01:38:28.980
But we got a bunch of discs and then we went
really high up on Highway 1 and threw them

01:38:28.980 --> 01:38:29.980
out into the ocean.

01:38:29.980 --> 01:38:33.180
And my older brother doesn’t like this:
oh, you’re putting plastic in the ocean.

01:38:33.180 --> 01:38:40.640
But to watch a disc fly for about a minute,
it’s magical.

01:38:40.640 --> 01:38:42.610
In yoga there’s this philosophy, Swaha.

01:38:42.610 --> 01:38:45.910
I call it fuck it, whatever; let go.

01:38:45.910 --> 01:38:51.520
So when I throw discs – I’ve thrown it
off Machu Picchu, I threw it off Chichen Itza;

01:38:51.520 --> 01:38:53.460
they did not like it in Chichen Itza.

01:38:53.460 --> 01:38:56.260
But I like to throw Frisbees off of really
high objects.

01:38:56.260 --> 01:39:01.190
And when I’m in these very ceremonial places
like Machu Picchu, it’s like what am I releasing?

01:39:01.190 --> 01:39:02.699
So it’s an intentional act.

01:39:02.699 --> 01:39:07.030
Tim Ferriss: It’s like burning something
on a piece of paper in the flames of a fire.

01:39:07.030 --> 01:39:08.030
Jason Nemer: Yeah.

01:39:08.030 --> 01:39:09.520
But then you get to watch it fly.

01:39:09.520 --> 01:39:12.880
Tim Ferriss: Then you get to watch it fly.

01:39:12.880 --> 01:39:15.790
What do you believe that other people think
is insane?

01:39:15.790 --> 01:39:18.060
Jason Nemer: That you can trust people.

01:39:18.060 --> 01:39:19.380
You can trust a lot of people.

01:39:19.380 --> 01:39:21.730
You don’t have to live in fear of strangers.

01:39:21.730 --> 01:39:25.840
Strangers are just people you haven’t flown,
yet.

01:39:25.840 --> 01:39:28.170
I’ve been all over the world.

01:39:28.170 --> 01:39:31.590
My mom was not happy when I was going to go
to the Middle East for the first time.

01:39:31.590 --> 01:39:35.560
I was actually in Boston, about to lead a
teacher training and this is when the Boston

01:39:35.560 --> 01:39:37.890
Marathon bomber happened.

01:39:37.890 --> 01:39:40.980
I had 15 students that were on lockdown for
24 hours.

01:39:40.980 --> 01:39:45.460
And I got on the phone to my mom and I’m
like, look, Mom, you think Israel is dangerous,

01:39:45.460 --> 01:39:46.830
I’m in Boston.

01:39:46.830 --> 01:39:49.670
You cannot hide from danger.

01:39:49.670 --> 01:39:51.639
But I don’t think that’s a reason to not
trust people.

01:39:51.639 --> 01:39:53.250
I’m a very trusting person.

01:39:53.250 --> 01:39:56.929
I’ve traveled the world in some very sketchy
places and I’ve never had anything bad happen

01:39:56.929 --> 01:39:58.250
to me.

01:39:58.250 --> 01:40:00.000
I assume the best in people.

01:39:58.250 --> 01:40:00.000
I assume the best in people.

01:40:00.000 --> 01:40:03.480
I assume that I can trust them until they
prove me wrong.

01:40:03.480 --> 01:40:06.599
I think a lot of people think that’s crazy.

01:40:06.599 --> 01:40:11.330
But when you do this practice enough, trusting
is like a muscle that you flex.

01:40:11.330 --> 01:40:12.900
And it doesn’t mean that I’m a cowboy
with it.

01:40:12.900 --> 01:40:16.130
Like I have really good credit assessment.

01:40:16.130 --> 01:40:20.900
I can tell is this person trustworthy for
this engagement I’m about to have.

01:40:20.900 --> 01:40:25.460
But just in general, having an open mind,
having an open heart, believing that people

01:40:25.460 --> 01:40:28.040
have good intentions.

01:40:28.040 --> 01:40:32.449
Tim Ferriss: How much of that do you think
is bad things not happening to you versus

01:40:32.449 --> 01:40:34.890
seeing things in the most positive light?

01:40:34.890 --> 01:40:37.580
Because you had your throwing knives lifted
in Panama.

01:40:37.580 --> 01:40:39.900
So shit happens, I’m imagining.

01:40:39.900 --> 01:40:46.159
Jason Nemer: Well, one of the things that
happened to me that was really amazing was

01:40:46.159 --> 01:40:48.770
I had all of my objects liberated from me.

01:40:48.770 --> 01:40:54.369
I was living in a van in San Francisco, and
I had my 30th birthday party.

01:40:54.369 --> 01:40:55.460
Basically I didn’t want to work in restaurants.

01:40:55.460 --> 01:40:59.210
It’s like I’m a yogi, I’m going to do
this; I don’t care how hard it is, I love

01:40:59.210 --> 01:41:00.250
this, boom.

01:41:00.250 --> 01:41:01.290
So I was living in my van.

01:41:01.290 --> 01:41:04.750
My 30th birthday, my friend throws me a party.

01:41:04.750 --> 01:41:08.160
That night I got a book on Buddhism, a case
of coconuts, I hung out with my friends.

01:41:08.160 --> 01:41:10.199
The next day, my van’s gone.

01:41:10.199 --> 01:41:11.690
My home is gone.

01:41:11.690 --> 01:41:13.090
Everything is gone.

01:41:13.090 --> 01:41:17.000
So I go crack a coconut and start reading
about Buddhism because what the fuck else

01:41:17.000 --> 01:41:18.510
am I gonna do?

01:41:18.510 --> 01:41:22.030
And page 4 is talking about homelessness and
wandering.

01:41:22.030 --> 01:41:24.050
I’m like, that’s what I’m going to do.

01:41:24.050 --> 01:41:26.870
And that started my nomadic traveling.

01:41:26.870 --> 01:41:31.100
And if I had stayed in San Francisco and tried
to make it as a yoga teacher, AcroYoga wouldn’t

01:41:31.100 --> 01:41:32.380
be a worldwide practice.

01:41:32.380 --> 01:41:38.410
So the ability to let go of what’s not working
and really assess what is working and what

01:41:38.410 --> 01:41:40.880
can I be excited about, it’s true.

01:41:40.880 --> 01:41:42.830
It’s not that bad things don’t happen
to me.

01:41:42.830 --> 01:41:45.370
I don’t label a lot of things good/bad.

01:41:45.370 --> 01:41:46.640
Can I evolve from this?

01:41:46.640 --> 01:41:47.640
What do I want now?

01:41:47.640 --> 01:41:48.640
Where is my center now?

01:41:48.640 --> 01:41:49.640
And this is all from yoga.

01:41:49.640 --> 01:41:50.750
Well, not all.

01:41:50.750 --> 01:41:55.790
My parents, a lot of other things but yoga
is the daily practice that helps me bring

01:41:55.790 --> 01:41:58.650
this wisdom into action steps in my life.

01:41:58.650 --> 01:42:02.480
Tim Ferriss: You mentioned something that
I’ve never heard you talk about, and we’ve

01:42:02.480 --> 01:42:06.580
never talked about before, but the restaurants
and then the decision, fuck it; I’m gonna

01:42:06.580 --> 01:42:08.440
do this full time.

01:42:08.440 --> 01:42:12.230
Walk me through the 24 hours that led to that
decision.

01:42:12.230 --> 01:42:16.310
At the time, did you realize it was financially
feasible?

01:42:16.310 --> 01:42:22.620
Or was it just a Hail Mary, this is what I’m
meant to do?

01:42:22.620 --> 01:42:25.390
Walk me through the 24 hours before you’re
like, alright, I’m pulling the trigger;

01:42:25.390 --> 01:42:26.500
this is what I’m doing.

01:42:26.500 --> 01:42:31.790
Jason Nemer: I made up my resume for restaurants,
and I was living in San Francisco.

01:42:31.790 --> 01:42:35.310
We had a rent-controlled house that three
yogis were living in because the people that

01:42:35.310 --> 01:42:38.370
had the rent control left town.

01:42:38.370 --> 01:42:43.820
I was dirt bagging it, but at that point,
all I needed was yoga and food.

01:42:43.820 --> 01:42:44.910
I didn’t need money.

01:42:44.910 --> 01:42:46.360
I didn’t have other ambitions.

01:42:46.360 --> 01:42:47.679
I wasn’t traveling the world.

01:42:47.679 --> 01:42:52.820
So if I had enough money to take yoga classes
and to eat well, that’s all I needed.

01:42:52.820 --> 01:42:57.150
When I got to that point of do I get a job,
or do I really dedicate to the yoga thing,

01:42:57.150 --> 01:42:59.619
it really wasn’t a choice.

01:42:59.619 --> 01:43:04.230
It was such a big thing that happened in my
life when yoga really hit me over the head;

01:43:04.230 --> 01:43:08.889
this is all I want to do and if I do these
other things, I’m not being true to the

01:43:08.889 --> 01:43:10.120
practices that I’ve been learning about.

01:43:10.120 --> 01:43:13.449
Like this is time for me to really dedicate
to this.

01:43:13.449 --> 01:43:17.040
My mom had a van, and she didn’t need it.

01:43:17.040 --> 01:43:22.170
I never thought I would live in a van but
San Francisco was, and still is, super competitive

01:43:22.170 --> 01:43:23.230
with yoga.

01:43:23.230 --> 01:43:26.940
Yoga teachers are some of the most underpaid
people on the planet because they have the

01:43:26.940 --> 01:43:32.100
potential to really bring a lot of beauty
to people’s lives, and they get paid $30

01:43:32.100 --> 01:43:35.090
to $100 a class, depending on what city you’re
in.

01:43:35.090 --> 01:43:41.320
So if I didn’t live in the van, there really
weren’t a lot of other solutions I saw,

01:43:41.320 --> 01:43:44.380
until the van was liberated and then I saw
the other solutions.

01:43:44.380 --> 01:43:50.460
Living on the road, you’re never really
successful, at least I haven’t been, in

01:43:50.460 --> 01:43:52.320
the cities where you start things.

01:43:52.320 --> 01:43:57.220
You go further away from where things started
and you’re more luxurious and more exotic.

01:43:57.220 --> 01:44:01.430
San Francisco is still a difficult city to
fill a room for me.

01:44:01.430 --> 01:44:06.060
So if I would have kept hitting my head against
that wall, I wouldn’t be here, the practice

01:44:06.060 --> 01:44:07.060
wouldn’t be here.

01:44:07.060 --> 01:44:08.250
Tim Ferriss: I love it.

01:44:08.250 --> 01:44:09.300
I love that.

01:44:09.300 --> 01:44:13.540
We’re going to wrap up with just a few last
questions.

01:44:13.540 --> 01:44:18.520
I suppose this is as good a place as any to
ask if you had a billboard and could put anything

01:44:18.520 --> 01:44:22.360
on it, what would you put on it?

01:44:22.360 --> 01:44:24.170
Jason Nemer: Play!

01:44:24.170 --> 01:44:25.170
Play more.

01:44:25.170 --> 01:44:26.170
Play.

01:44:26.170 --> 01:44:27.170
Yeah.

01:44:27.170 --> 01:44:28.310
I feel like people are so serious.

01:44:28.310 --> 01:44:37.640
And it doesn’t take much for people to drop
back into the wisdom of a childlike playfulness.

01:44:37.640 --> 01:44:44.429
I think if I had to prescribe two things to
improve health and happiness in the world,

01:44:44.429 --> 01:44:47.030
it’s movement and play, those two things.

01:44:47.030 --> 01:44:53.480
Because you can’t really play without moving
so they’re kind of intertwined.

01:44:53.480 --> 01:44:56.510
But if you’re just moving and you’re not
enjoying yourself as you’re moving.

01:44:56.510 --> 01:44:59.770
If you’re on a treadmill, treadmills kill
your spirit.

01:44:59.770 --> 01:45:03.860
There are reasons and times to do treadmills,
but if that is your only way of moving your

01:45:03.860 --> 01:45:05.770
body, you’re selling yourself short.

01:45:05.770 --> 01:45:10.560
There are way cooler ways to move your body;
way more fun things and I just happened to

01:45:10.560 --> 01:45:14.380
have the good fortune to learn a lot of these
really cool things.

01:45:14.380 --> 01:45:15.630
So play.

01:45:15.630 --> 01:45:17.500
Tim Ferriss: Awesome.

01:45:17.500 --> 01:45:20.889
Jason, where can people find you online?

01:45:20.889 --> 01:45:22.580
What would you like them to check out?

01:45:22.580 --> 01:45:29.619
Jason Nemer: Acroyoga.org is the website and
there are free learning opportunities.

01:45:29.619 --> 01:45:33.080
AcroYoga International is the name of the
company that I run.

01:45:33.080 --> 01:45:37.070
And on YouTube, you can go there and see a
bunch of free videos that get you started.

01:45:37.070 --> 01:45:38.680
Tim Ferriss: What is the handle on YouTube?

01:45:38.680 --> 01:45:40.410
Jason Nemer: AcroYoga International.

01:45:40.410 --> 01:45:41.840
Tim Ferriss: Okay, AcroYoga International.

01:45:41.840 --> 01:45:46.910
And we’ll put all this in the show notes,
folks so you can find everything else.

01:45:46.910 --> 01:45:50.590
Anything on social you’d like to mention;
Instagram, Twitter, Facebook?

01:45:50.590 --> 01:45:51.590
Where are you?

01:45:51.590 --> 01:45:53.280
Jason Nemer: It’s all AcroYoga and it’s
all Jason Nemer.

01:45:53.280 --> 01:45:56.250
So JasonNemer.com, JasonNemer on Instagram.

01:45:56.250 --> 01:46:00.260
I try to keep it simple because I like to
keep things simple if I can.

01:46:00.260 --> 01:46:01.699
Tim Ferriss: Awesome, man.

01:46:01.699 --> 01:46:02.699
So much fun.

01:46:02.699 --> 01:46:03.699
Always great to hang.

01:46:03.699 --> 01:46:04.699
Jason Nemer: Right on.

01:46:04.699 --> 01:46:06.410
Tim Ferriss: More flying and basing in the
future.

01:46:06.410 --> 01:46:09.080
Jason Nemer: What a weird grip.

01:46:09.080 --> 01:46:11.560
That’s not very useful.

01:46:11.560 --> 01:46:13.530
There we go.

01:46:13.530 --> 01:46:14.530
Good stuff.

01:46:14.530 --> 01:46:15.530
Thank you, Tim.

01:46:15.530 --> 01:46:16.530
Tim Ferriss: Thanks.

01:46:16.530 --> 01:46:21.040
Everybody listening and everybody watching,
as always, you can find the show notes at

01:46:21.040 --> 01:46:22.130
fourhourworkweek.com/podcast.

01:46:22.130 --> 01:46:25.240
I’ll have links to everything that we talked
about.

01:46:25.240 --> 01:46:40.239
And until next time, thank you for listening
and thank you for watching.

01:46:40.239 --> 01:46:41.480
Signing off.

01:46:41.480 --> 01:46:45.239
Jason Nemer: Ya, mon.

01:46:45.239 --> 01:46:49.000
Tim Ferriss: Ya, mon.

01:46:49.000 --> 01:47:17.430
Jason Nemer: That was fun.

